1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giovanni_Visconti_(archbishop_of_Milan)_
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Giovanni Visconti''' (1290–1354) was an Italian Roman Catholic cardinal, who was co-ruler in Milan and lord of other Italian cities. He also was a military leader who fought against Florence, and used force to capture and hold 
2 CONC other cities.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Matteo I Visconti and Bonacossa Borri.
2 CONT
2 CONT Giovanni Visconti was elected archbishop by the Capitol of Milan in 1317, but Pope John XXII refused to confirm the election and instead raised Aicardus from Comodeia to that position. In 1323 John excommunicated him with an accus
2 CONC ation of heresy, and Visconti found an ally in the antipope Nicholas V, who give him the title of cardinal. In 1331 he became bishop and lord of Novara, and in 1339, after Aicardus' death, he triumphantly entered Milan, although P
2 CONC ope Clement VI only issued a bull confirming him in the archbishopric in 1342. Officially, he thus was Archbishop of Milan from 1342 to 1354.
2 CONT
2 CONT Together with his brother Luchino, Visconti bought from the Pope the title of co-ruler of Milan, for 500,000 florins. After Luchino's death, he associated in the lordship the sons of his other brother, Stefano, who were Matteo II,
2 CONC  Bernabò and Galeazzo II.
2 CONT
2 CONT The year after Luchino Visconti's death in 1349, and with the approval of his relations, Giovanni Visconti assumed full lordship of Milan and began consolidating power in Lombardy and beyond. The same year, 1350, he obtained lords
2 CONC hip over Bologna and placed his nephew, Bernabò, in charge of the city in 1351.
2 CONT
2 CONT Afraid of his growing strength, in 1350 Florence organized a conference in Arezzo with a papal legate and representatives of other cities to form an alliance against Milan. Aware of these moves against him, Giovanni Visconti culti
2 CONC vated affection and alliance with the Ghibellines of Tuscany and Romagna. After the death of Mastino II della Scala of Verona, who had been hostile to the Archbishop, he gained the friendship of Mastino’s son, Cangrande II della S
2 CONC cala.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1351, he sent troops from Milan and Bologna, and from allies in Faenza and Forlì, all led by Bernabò, to lay siege to Imola. With war occurring in the Romagna region, Giovanni Visconti was able to lull the Florentines into beli
2 CONC eving that he had no intentions towards them. However, he then had many leading Bolognese citizens arrested and tortured, and extracted confessions from them of a conspiracy with Florence to overthrow his rule. He used this as a j
2 CONC ustification of war against Florence and the Guelphs of Tuscany. The Archbishop placed Giovanni da Oleggio, another Visconti, in command, and he amassed an army from Bologna and led them into Tuscany to besiege and capture towns a
2 CONC nd castles, while Ghibelline allies in Tuscany wreaked havoc elsewhere in the region.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1352, Giovanni Visconti became Lord of Genoa, and in the following year, he added Novara. In 1353, Petrarch visited as his guest.
2 CONT
2 CONT Giovanni Visconti died 5 October 1354.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------
