1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Gravesite information: https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/142110400/maddalena-visconti
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT English: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maddalena_Visconti
2 CONT
2 CONT Italiano: http://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maddalena_Visconti
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Maddalena Visconti
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Maddalena Visconti (1366 – 17 July 1404) was a daughter of Bernabò Visconti and his wife Beatrice Regina della Scala. Maddalena was Duchess of Bavaria-Landshut by her marriage to Frederick, Duke of Bavaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Maddalena was born in Milan and was the twelfth of seventeen children born to her parents.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maddalena's sister, Taddea Visconti married Stephen III, Duke of Bavaria and was mother of Isabeau of Bavaria, wife of Charles VI of France. Isabeau was mother of Charles VII of France, Catherine, Queen of England, Isabella, Queen
2 CONC  of England and Michelle, Duchess of Valois. Another sister, Agnes, married Francesco I Gonzaga and was executed for supposed adultery in 1391. Maddalena's youngest sister Elisabetta was married to John II, Duke of Bavaria. Elisab
2 CONC etta was Duchess of Bavaria-Munich, so was one of Maddalena's rivals as well as elder sister Taddea.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maddalena's maternal grandparents were Mastino II della Scala and his wife Taddea da Carrara. Her paternal grandparents were Stefano Visconti and his wife Valentina Doria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father, Bernabò was a cruel and ruthless despot, and an implacable enemy of the Church. He seized the papal city of Bologna, rejected the Pope and his authority, confiscated ecclesiastical property, and forbade any of his subj
2 CONC ects to have any dealings with the Curia. He was excommunicated as a heretic in 1363 by Pope Urban V, who preached crusade against him.[1] When Bernabò was in one of his frequent rages, only Beatrice Regina (her mother) was able t
2 CONC o approach him.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Maddalena married on 2 September 1381 Frederick, Duke of Bavaria. Her father wanted to improve relations with Bavaria so married Maddalena, Elisabetta and Taddea to the three rival Dukes. Frederick had been previously married to A
2 CONC nna of Neuffen, with whom he had a daughter, Isabella of Bavaria, but no sons. On Anna's death, Frederick married Maddalena.
2 CONT
2 CONT The couple had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry XVI the Rich (1386–1450).
2 CONT
2 CONT Johann, died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth (1383–13 November 1442, Ansbach), married to Frederick I, Margrave of Brandenburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margareta (b. 1384), died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT Magdalene (1388–1410), married 1404 to Count Johann Meinhard VII of Görz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maddalena's stepdaughter, Isabella was married to one of Maddalena's relatives, Marco Visconti, Lord of Parma.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1375 to 1392 Frederick ruled with his brothers Stephen III and John II, Maddalena therefor ruled as consort jointly with sister Taddea, but only for a few weeks, before Taddea died. John II's wife was Katharina of Görz, it is
2 CONC  unknown whether Maddalena and Katharina served as consort at the same time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick managed to administer the richest part of the duchy, Lower Bavaria-Landshut which he also kept after the division of Bavaria among the brothers in 1392 when Bavaria-Ingolstadt and Bavaria-Munich were created.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1387 Frederick imprisoned the archbishop of Salzburg to force him to finish his alliance with a confederation of cities in Swabia. Frederick was an advisor of King Wenceslaus in legal affairs and a favorable candidate for the k
2 CONC ing's succession when he died at Budweis already in 1393. He was succeeded in Bavaria-Landshut by his son with Maddalena Henry, leaving Maddalena a widow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry inherited not only the black hair of Maddalena but also the despotic temperament of her family, Visconti, Henry oppressed very cruelly uprisings of the citizenry of Landshut in 1410 and fought successfully against his cousin
2 CONC  Louis VII the Bearded, the duke of Bavaria-Ingolstadt. He united Louis’ enemies in the Parakeet Society of 1414 and the League of Constance of 1415.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maddalena died in Bavaria 17 July 1404. She is buried at Kloster Raitenhaslach.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Daugther of Bernabò Viscontis and Beatrice della Scala. She married Friedrich von Bayern-Landshut in 1381.
