1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francesco_II_Gonzaga,_Marquis_of_Mantua
2 CONT
2 CONT Francesco II Gonzaga (10 August 1466 – 29 March 1519) was Marquis of Mantua from 1484 until his death. A prominent condottiero and statesman during the Italian Wars, he is best known for commanding the Italian league forces at the
2 CONC  Battle of Fornovo in 1495. Through his marriage to Isabella d'Este, one of the leading women of the Italian Renaissance, Francesco helped establish Mantua as a significant cultural and artistic center.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT Born in Mantua, Francesco was the son of Marquis Federico I Gonzaga and Margaret of Bavaria.[1] He started a career as a condottiero acting as Venice's commander from 1489 to 1498. Francesco was the commander-in-chief of the army 
2 CONC of the Italian league in the battle of Fornovo, under the tutorage of his more experienced uncle Ridolfo Gonzaga: even though Francesco was unable to stop Charles VIII and his army from returning to France,[2] he claimed Fornovo a
2 CONC s a victory.[3] He commissioned Andrea Mantegna’s Madonna della Vittoria to honor his self-proclaimed victory at Fornovo.[4] Francesco was described as "short, pop-eyed, snub-nosed and exceptionally brave, and was regarded as the 
2 CONC finest knight in Italy".[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Francesco briefly commanded the Venetian army,[3] but in 1502 he left to pay his respects to Louis XII who was then at Milan.[6] By 29 April, he was with Louis XII when Genoa fell to the French army.[6] Francesco, taking the initi
2 CONC ative after the French victory at Agnadello, was occupying lands that he had lost to Venice.[7] He was marching to Legnago with a company of French lances, when he was captured by the Venetians.[8] Francesco only gained his freedo
2 CONC m by giving his son Federico II as hostage.[9] Once free, he was placed in command of papal troops, although he was ineffective since he stayed in Mantua. By 17 February 1510, Francesco, no longer involved in the war,[10] allowed 
2 CONC a French army under Gaston of Foix to march through his territory to reach Brescia.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT During Francesco's absences, Mantua was governed by his wife Isabella d'Este,[12] whom he had married on 12 February 1490. Under their reign, Mantua knew a great age of cultural splendour, with the presence in the city of artists 
2 CONC such as Andrea Mantegna and Jacopo Bonacolsi. Francesco had the Palace of St. Sebastian built, where Mantegna's Triumph of Caesar was eventually placed. The Palace was where Francesco lived when in Mantua. His wife, Isabella d'Est
2 CONC e remained at the Castello di San Giorgio where she had her own suite of rooms. On completing the decoration of his rooms at the palace, Francesco asked his wife for her views. Isabella commented favourably, though she did say tha
2 CONC t the decorations were almost as good as those within her studiolo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beginning in 1503, he started a long relationship with Lucrezia Borgia, including using coded letters.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 29 March 1519, Francesco, who had suffered from syphilis, died.[14] He was succeeded by his son Federico, with Isabella acting as regent. Another son, Ferrante Gonzaga originated the branch of the Counts of Guastalla.
2 CONT
2 CONT Loves
2 CONT Francesco was known for his passion for women, so much so that, on the occasion of the siege of Novara in 1495, his sister-in-law Beatrice d'Este, wanting to ingratiate herself with him, offered to personally procure him a "femmin
2 CONC a di partito" with whom to celebrate the victory, under the pretext of protecting both him and his wife and sister Isabella from malfrancese.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis also actively practiced sodomy, according to the ancient Greek custom, very widespread almost everywhere at that time, as he himself proudly claims in his poisonous letter of accusations to Galeazzo Sanseverino, dated 1503
2 CONC : "I am reputed and raised by nobility of birth and good morals; you for human and ass favors (and I usually have a party at the door of others, and not at mine!)".[16]
2 CONT
2 CONT It is well known that he surrounded himself with ruffians and mezzani, who had the task of procuring for him girls and young ephebes. One of these was, among others, Ludovico Camposampiero [it], who therefore ran into the hatred o
2 CONC f the Marquise.[17]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT Isabella d'Este and Francesco Gonzaga had:
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleonora Gonzaga, born 1493, died 1570. Married Francesco Maria I della Rovere Duke of Urbino[18]
2 CONT Margherita, born 1496.
