2 CONT Parents	Louis XII
2 CONT Education	University of Orleans
2 CONT Michel Bucy (1484 – 8 February 1511) was the archbishop of Bourges, and the illegitimate son of Louis XII.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth and early life
2 CONT Michel Bucy was born in 1484 to Louis d'Orléans and an unknown mother. Bucy's mother has been the subject of debate: while some speculate that his mother was a count laundress, others believe that his mother was from the House of 
2 CONC Amboises given that the Amboises controlled the lands of Bussy (Bucy).[2][3] Bucy attended the University of Orleans.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Career
2 CONT At 21, Bucy was named the archbishop of Bourges by his father in November 1505.[4][5] Although it was standard practice at the time for kings to name underaged individuals from important families as prelates, Louis XII was relativ
2 CONC ely conservative in this practice, which highlights Bucy as a notable exception.[4] At the onset, Bucy's selection was controversial among the deans, canons, and the chapter as one had to be at least 35 to become an archbishop.[6]
2 CONC  Nonetheless, the vast majority of the chapter supported Bucy because of his royal backing, with only one member advocating for a fair election. At this time, Bucy's status as Louis XII's son was not publicly known.[3][6] Eventual
2 CONC ly, Bucy received the chapter's overwhelming support and votes – with one abstention.[6] Bucy then declared that it was up to the Pope to judge his qualifications to become archbishop. In his letters to the Pope, Bucy described hi
2 CONC s position as the illegitimate son of Louis XII and noted that when asked by his father which profession he would be interested in, Bucy – supposedly inspired by Saint Sulpice – chose the Catholic Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bucy officially became archbishop on 22 February, 1506.[6] Although Bucy technically could not become an archbishop due to his young age, he was formally inducted and is generally given that title by the Catholic Church and biogra
2 CONC phers of his father.[1][5] In 1509, Bucy consecrated the Church of Saint-Aignan after six additional chapels were added to its nave and other extensive reconstruction efforts were completed.[7][8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and Resting Place
2 CONT Bucy died on 8 February 1511.[9] He is buried by the Bourges Cathedral, near the chancellor's stall and next to the archbishopric – the archbishop's break room. On his tombstone's epithet, he is only referred to as the patriarch a
2 CONC nd primate of the Aquitaines.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.amis-cathedrale-bourges.com/histoire/archev%C3%AAques/bucy/
2 CONT * https://catholic-hierarchy.org/bishop/bbucy.html
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/224916093/michel-de_bucy
2 CONT
2 CONT Michel de Bucy was the illegitimate son of King Louis XII by unknown mistress.
2 CONT Protonotary apostolic and doyen of Saint-Aignan at Orléans. Proposed as Archbishop of Bourges 25 Sep 1505, his appointment was confirmed by Pope Julius II 21 Nov 1505.
