1 NAME Louis /de Brézé/ d'Anet
2 GIVN Louis
2 NSFX d'Anet
2 _MARNM de Brézé
1 NAME Louis de Brézé, comte de Maulévrier
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Chapelle de la Vierge de la cathédrale de Rouen
2 ADDR
3 CITY Rouen
3 STAE Upper Normandy
3 CTRY France
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1463
1 DEAT
2 DATE 23 JUL 1531
2 PLAC Anet, 28, France
2 ADDR
1 OCCU Comte de Maulévrier (76), Gd Sénéchal de Normandie (1490)
1 TITL seigneur
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Louis de Brézé, Seigneur d'Anet and Comte de Maulevrier''' (died 23 July 1531) was a French nobleman, the grandson of King Charles VII of France by his natural daughter with his mistress Agnès Sorel.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Early life'''
2 CONT Louis was the son of Jacques de Brézé, Sénéchal of Normandy, and Charlotte de Valois, the second of Charles VII's three daughters by Agnès Sorel.[1] His paternal grandfather was Pierre de Brézé, noted for valour at Formigny, a gra
2 CONC nd steward of Normandy.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Career'''
2 CONT In 1523, Louis uncovered a plot against King Francis I.[2] He did not know at the time that his father-in-law, Jean de Poitiers, Seigneur de Saint Vallier, was involved in the plot.[2] Jean was condemned to death over that involve
2 CONC ment, but reprieved by the king due to his having no direct involvement.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Personal life'''
2 CONT Louis was influential at court, being named Sénéchal of Normandy and Master of the Hunt. His home was the family seat, the Château d'Anet, which stood in a royal hunting preserve in the valley of the Eure. He also inherited the Ch
2 CONC âteau du Bec-Crespin.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Before his death in 1531, Louis encouraged the marriage of Prince Henry to the Pope's great-niece, Catherine de' Medici, thus setting up the triangle that was to continue until Henry's death, with his widow, Diane de Poitiers, bec
2 CONC oming Henry's mistress. For Diane, King Henry II rebuilt the old Château d'Anet, which became one of the first French Renaissance châteaus, and she would be entrusted with much of the management of royal court business.[citation n
2 CONC eeded]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriage and children'''
2 CONT Louis' first wife was Catherine de Dreux. Later, he married Diane de Poitiers on 29 March 1515; she was 15, and he 39 years older.[4] They had:
2 CONT
2 CONT Françoise de Brézé,(b.1518)[2] married Robert IV de La Marck[5]
2 CONT Louise de Brézé,(b.1521)[2] married Claude, Duke of Aumale[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Death and legacy'''
2 CONT Louis died on 23 July 1531.[6] It has been expressly stated in an old Norman manuscript, that his bowels were interred at Anet, his heart in the abbey of Coulombs near his father, and his body carried to Notre Dame at Rouen and pl
2 CONC aced near that of his grandfather Pierre. The tomb that his widow Diane erected for Louis in the cathedral of Rouen was one of the early projects of French Renaissance sculptor Jean Goujon.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_de_Br%C3%A9z%C3%A9
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Individual Note===
2 CONT Il reçut tous les biens de son père Jacques après la condamnation de celui-ci pour l'assassinat de sa femme Charlotte de Valois-Sorel la mère de Louis.
