2 TITL John Frederick I, Elector of Saxony
1 NOTE ELECTOR OF SAXONY; KNOWN AS "THE MAGNANIMOUS"; TAKEN PRISONER AT THE BATTLE
2 CONT NEAR MULBERG 4/24/1547, AND DEPRIVED OF THE ELECTORATE
1 NOTE In 1526, one year after the death of Friedrich the Wise, the wedding of his nephew, Johann Friedrich I., known as the Magnanimous, took place. His bride was Sibylle of Cleve, who was only fourteen years old at the time. The marria
2 CONC ge represented something approaching a union between the Electorate of Saxony and the Duchy of Jⁿlich-Kleve-Berg and, although arranged for political and strategic reasons, it is said to have proved a particularly happy one. Much 
2 CONC less successful was Johann FriedrichΓÇÖs subsequent political career. He was defeated in 1547 at the battle of Mⁿhlberg during the Schmalkaldic War. As a result, he was stripped of his electorship and spent several years as a pris
2 CONC oner. When his ducal title was finally restored in 1552, however, Johann Friedrich returned to his new seat in Weimar, accompanied by his former court painter Lucas Cranach the Elder, and was triumphantly received by the populatio
2 CONC n. For Weimar, this event represented the beginning of a unique period of cultural ascendancy.
2 CONT The portraits of the young bridal couple which hang in the Schlossmuseum are exquisite examples of the mature portrait style of Lucas Cranach the Elder. The painter combines courtly grandeur with aclear emphasis on individuality t
2 CONC o produce a likeness of the subjects which is striking in its simplicity.
