1 OCCU Jacqueline de Longwy, Countess of Bar-sur-Seine
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Jacqueline de Longwy
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacqueline de Longwy, Countess of Bar-sur-Seine (before 1520- 28 August 1561), Duchess of Montpensier, Dauphine of Auvergne was a French noblewoman, and a half-niece of King Francis I of France. She was the first wife of Louis III
2 CONC  de Bourbon, Duke of Montpensier, and the mother of his six children. Charlotte of Bourbon, the third wife of William The Silent, Prince of Orange, was her daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacqueline was born on an unknown date sometime before 1520, the youngest daughter of Jean IV de Longwy, Seigneur de Givry, Baron of Pagny and of Mirebeau (died 1520) and Jeanne of Angouleme, Countess of Bar-sur-Seine (c.1490- aft
2 CONC er 1531/1538), the illegitimate half-sister of King Francis I of France.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacqueline had two older sisters. The eldest, Francoise de Longwy, Dame de Pagny and de Mirebeau (c.1510- after 14 April 1561), married firstly in 1526, Philippe de Chabot, Seigneur De Brion, Count of Charny and Buzançois, Admiral
2 CONC  of France, by whom she had six children, and secondly in 1545, Jacques de Perusse, Seigneur d'Escars, by whom she had a son, Cardinal Anne d'Escars de Givry. Her second eldest sister was Claude Louise de Longwy, Abbess of Jouarre
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacqueline's paternal uncle was Claude de Longwy de Givry, Bishop of Amiens. Her paternal grandparents were Philippe de Longwy, Seigneur of Pagny and Jeanne de Bauffremont, Dame de Mirebeau. Her maternal grandparents were Charles,
2 CONC  Count of Angouleme and his mistress Antoinette de Polignac, Dame de Combronde, who was a lady-in-waiting to the Count of Angouleme's wife, Louise of Savoy.[2]Antoinette (born c.1460) was the daughter of Foucaud de Polignac, Seign
2 CONC eur des Fontaines and Agnès de Chabanais.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1538, Jacqueline married, as his first wife, Louis III de Bourbon-Montpensier, who would become Duke of Montpensier on the death of his mother Louise de Bourbon, Duchess of Montpensier on 5 July 1561. On the occasion of the mar
2 CONC riage, Jacqueline's uncle, King Francis restored the Montpensier estates and the counties of Forez, Beaujeu, and Dombes to Louis' mother. They had been confiscated by the French Crown following Charles III, Duke of Bourbon's treas
2 CONC on in 1523 when he defected to the side of Emperor Charles V.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage of Jacqueline and Louis produced one son and five daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Francois, Duke of Montpensier (1542- 4 June 1592), married in 1566, Renee of Anjou, Marquise de Mézières (21 October 1550- 1597), daughter of Nicholas of Anjou, Marquis de Mézières and Gabrielle de Mareuil, by whom he had one son,
2 CONC  Henri de Bourbon, Duke of Montpensier.
2 CONT
2 CONT Francoise de Bourbon-Vendome (1539- 1587), in 1559 married Henri-Robert of La Marck (de la Marck), Duke of Bouillon, Prince of Sedan
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne de Bourbon (1540 1577), in 1561 married Francois II de Cleves, Duke of Nevers
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne de Bourbon, Abbess of Jouarre (1541- 1620)
2 CONT
2 CONT Charlotte de Bourbon (1547- 5 May 1582), on 24 June 1575, married William The Silent of Nassau, Prince of Orange, by whom she had six daughters
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise de Bourbon, Abbess of Faremoutier (1548- 1586)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacqueline succeeded to the title of suo jure Countess of Bar-sur-Seine upon her mother's death, which occurred on an unknown date sometime after 1531/1538. Her father's titles had passed to her eldest sister, Francoise upon his d
2 CONC eath in 1520.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1543, the dauphinate of Auvergne was restored to Louis, Jacqueline therefore became the Dauphine of Auvergne.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in Paris on 28 August 1561. She had been the Duchess of Montpensier for less than two months.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband married secondly, in 1570, Catherine of Lorraine, daughter of Francis, Duke of Guise and Anna d'Este. Their marriage was childless.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through her daughter, Charlotte, Jacqueline was an ancestress of the House of Hanover, which reigned in Great Britain from 1714 to 1901, and from which descends the current British Royal Family.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]In art
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacqueline de Longwy's portrait was drawn in 1550 by French painter Francois Clouet.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Notes
