2 CONT
2 CONT Giovanni passò la propria infanzia in un convento, poiché la madre era prigioniera di [https://www.geni.com/people/Cesare-Borgia-duc-de-Valentinois/6000000000350950665  Cesare Borgia]. Nel 1509 Caterina Sforza morì, ed essendo mor
2 CONC to anche Luffo Numai, primo tutore di Giovanni, la tutela del giovane passò al canonico Francesco Fortunati e al ricchissimo fiorentino [https://www.geni.com/people/Jacopo-Salviati/6000000001893607236  Jacopo Salviati], marito di 
2 CONC [https://www.geni.com/people/Lucrezia-Salviati/6000000001893602506   Lucrezia de' Medici], figlia di [https://www.geni.com/people/Lorenzo-de-Medici-il-Magnifico/6000000001063169161  Lorenzo il Magnifico].
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Giovanni de Medici
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #3219, b. 6 April 1498, d. 30 November 1526
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=30 Sep 2005
2 CONT
2 CONT      Giovanni de Medici was born on 6 April 1498. He was the son of unknown de Medici. ▼1 He married Mary Salviati, daughter of Giacomo Salviati and Lucrezia dei Medici, on 17 November 1516. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 30 November 1526 at age 28.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Giovanni de Medici also went by the nick-name of Giovanni 'the Invincible'.
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Giovanni de Medici and Mary Salviati
2 CONT
2 CONT -1. Cosimo I de Medici, Granduca di Toscana+ b. 12 Jun 1519, d. 21 Apr 1574
2 CONT
2 CONT Source / Forrás:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p322.htm#i3219
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Giovanni dalle Bande Nere
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Giovanni de' Medici, also known as Giovanni dalle Bande Nere (April 5, 1498 - November 30, 1526) was an Italian condottiero.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Giovanni was born in the Northern Italian town of Forlì to Giovanni de' Medici (also known as il Popolano) and Caterina Sforza, one of the most famous women of the Italian Renaissance.
2 CONT
2 CONT From an early age, he demonstrated great interest and ability in physical activity, especially the martial arts of the age: horse riding, sword-fighting, etc. He committed his first murder at the age of 12, and was twice banished 
2 CONC from the city of Florence for his unruly behavior. He married Maria Salviati, and had a son, Cosimo (1519-1574), who went on to become Grand Duke of Florence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Giovanni became a condottiero, or mercenary military captain, in the employ of Pope Leo X (Giovanni di Lorenzo de' Medici) and underwent his baptism by fire on March 5, 1516 in the war against Francesco Maria della Rovere, duke of
2 CONC  Urbino. Giovanni won after 22 days. He thenceforth formed a company of his own, mounted on light horses and specializing in fast but devastating skirmishing tactics and ambushes. In 1520 he defeated several rebel barons in the Ma
2 CONC rche. The following year Leo X allied with Emperor Charles V against King Francis I of France to regain Milan, Parma and Piacenza; Giovanni was called in under the command of Prospero Colonna, defeating the French at Vaprio d'Adda
2 CONC  in November.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a symbol of mourning for the death of Pope Leo X (December 1, 1521), Giovanni added black stripes to his insignia, whence comes his nick-name, Giovanni dalle Bande Nere (or Giovanni of the Black Bands). In the August 1523 he wa
2 CONC s hired by the Imperial army, and in January 1524 he defeated the French and the Swiss at Caprino Bergamasco. In the same year another Medici, Giulio di Giuliano, became Pope, and took the name of Clement VII. The new Pope paid al
2 CONC l of Giovanni's debt, but in exchange ordered him to switch to the French side of the ongoing conflict. He did not take part in the battle of Pavia, but was soon severely wounded in a skirmish and later had to move to Venice to be
2 CONC  cured.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1526 the War of the League of Cognac broke out. The League's captain general, Francesco della Rovere, abandoned Milan in the face of the overwhelming superiority of the Imperial army led by Georg von Frundsberg. Giovanni was ab
2 CONC le to defeat the Landsknechts rearguard at the confluence of the Mincio with the Po River.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Death
2 CONT
2 CONT On the evening of November 25 he was hit by a shot from a falconet in a battle near Govérnolo. The ball shattered his leg above the knee and he had to be carried to San Nicolò Po, where no doctor could be found. He was taken to Lu
2 CONC igi Gonzaga's palace in Mantua, where the surgeon Abramo, who had cared for him two years earlier, amputated his leg. To perform the operation Abramo asked for 10 men to hold down the stricken condottiero.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pietro Aretino, eyewitness to the event, recalled in a letter to Francesco Albizi:
2 CONT
2 CONT «Not even twenty» Giovanni said smiling «could hold me», and he took a candle in his hand, so that he could make light onto himself, I ran away, and shutting [sic] my ears I heard only two voices, and then calling, and when I reac
2 CONC hed him he told me: «I am healed», and turning all around he greatly rejoiced.[1] »
2 CONT
2 CONT Giovanni de' Medici died five days later, of septicemia, on November 30, 1526.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Giovanni's premature death metaphorically signaled the end of the age of the condottieri, as their mode of fighting (which emphasized armored knights on horseback) was rendered practically obsolete by the introduction of the mobil
2 CONC e field cannon. He is therefore known as the last of the great Italian condottieri. His lasting reputation has been kept alive in part thanks to Pietro Aretino, the Renaissance author, satirist, playwright and "scourge of the prin
2 CONC ces", who was Giovanni's close friend and accompanied him on some of his exploits.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later references
2 CONT
2 CONT A cruiser of the Regia Marina was named after Giovanni delle Bande Nere in 1930.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ermanno Olmi's 2001 film, Il mestiere delle armi, faithfully follows Giovanni dalle Bande Nere in his last week of life, as he engages in battle with the Imperial forces amidst the cold, damp fields of the Lombard countryside.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
