1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Alma Shelley''' born about 1490, daughter of John Shelly of Michelgrove, Sussex.  Married as his 1st wife to Sir Richard Sherley of Wiston, died 1540.  She died before  28 JUN 1525, the date of his 2nd marriage.
2 CONC   She was also known as Anne.
2 CONT
2 CONT children of Richard Shirley and Alma Shelly  
2 CONT # 4. (i) Thomas visitation says died sans issue
2 CONT # 4. (ii) John ( "2nd son" - source: visitation, not in other lists)
2 CONT # 4. (iii) Elizabeth m. John Mychell of Stammerham (visitation) (1540 Will)
2 CONT # 4. (iv) Ann married Richard Fernwold (visitation) (1540 Will)
2 CONT # 4. (v) Cicely married John Ledys (visitation)
2 CONT # 4. (vi) Alice married Thomas Chaunceler of Lyndford (visitation)
2 CONT # 4. (vii) Jane mentioned in father's 1540 Will as unmarried
2 CONT # 4. (viii) Frances mentioned in father's 1540 Will as unmarried
2 CONT # 4. (ix) Edward mentioned in father's 1540 Will as unmarried. Visitation says he died sans issue. Mentioned in brother William's 1551 Will
2 CONT # 4. (x) Richard Visitation says he died sans issue
2 CONT # 4. (xi) William Shirley b abt 1498 [ age 42 at time of father's death] d May 28, 1551. Buried in the chapel at Wiston. Will proved July 20, 1551 Prerogative Court of Canterbury. Heir of father in 1540 Will. He mentioned his brot
2 CONC her, Edward, and his sisters, Frances and Jane, in his will. m. Mary Isley
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT * MEM: Wiston, Sussex: Kneeling relief figure with husband
2 CONT
2 CONT ==comments==
2 CONT
2 CONT Not the daughter of [http://www.geni.com/people/John-Shelley-of-Michelgrove/6000000002447097086?through=6000000006444364022 John Shelley]  & [http://www.geni.com/people/Mary-Guildford/6000000002447097093?through=600000000644436402
2 CONC 2 Mary Fitzwilliams].  Some pedigrees appear to be in error. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://www.shirleyassociation.com/NewShirleySite/NonMembers/England/Lineages/wiston_pedigree.html
2 CONT * http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=ancestorsearch&id=I14515
2 CONT
2 CONT ___________________________
2 CONT *'''Anne Shelley
2 CONT *'''F, b. circa 1478
2 CONT *Father John Shelley, Esq. b. c 1455, d. 3 Jan 1526 
2 CONT *Mother Elizabeth Michelgrove b. c 1457, d. 30 Jun 1518 
2 CONT *'''     Anne Shelley was born circa 1478 at of Michelgrove, Sussex, England. She married Richard Shirley, son of Ralph Shirley and Jane Bellingham, circa 1498 at of Michelgrove, Sussex, England.
2 CONT *'''Family Richard Shirley b. c 1478, d. 10 Nov 1540 
2 CONT *Child 
2 CONT **William Shirley+ b. c 1515, d. 28 Feb 1551
2 CONT *From: http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p1707.htm#i51307 __________________
2 CONT *'''Peerage of England, genealogical, biographical, and historical (1812) Vol. IV.
2 CONT *https://archive.org/details/peerageofengland04colluoft
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/peerageofengland04colluoft#page/87/mode/1up
2 CONT * .... They derive their descent from SASUALLO or SEWALLUS de Etingdon, whose name (says Dugdale, in his Antiquities of Warwickshire), argues him to be of the old English stock ; which Se- 
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/peerageofengland04colluoft#page/88/mode/1up
2 CONT *wallis resided at Nether-Etingdon, in com. Warwick, about the reign of king Edward the Confessor : which place had been the seat of his ancestors, as there is reason to believe, for many generations before. After the Conquest, th
2 CONC e lordship of Etingdon was given to Henry Earl of Ferrars, in Normandy, who was one of the principal adventurers with the Norman Duke William, and was held under him by this Sewallus ; to whose posterity, .... etc.   He died about
2 CONC  1085. It appears by Kenilworth Register, that he built and endowed the church of Etingdon. 
2 CONT *   FULCHER, his only child, succeeded him; and died about 1105, leaving issue, 1. Sewallus. 2. Henry, from whom the Shirleys of Ireton, Co. Derby, who took the name of Ireton. 3. Fulcher, twice married, but died S. P. 4. Nicholas
2 CONC . 5. Robert. 
2 CONT *   SEWALLUS died about 1129 ; leaving by his first wife, Matilda, daughter of Ridel, of Halaughton, Co. Derb. 1. Henry. 2. Ful- 
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/peerageofengland04colluoft#page/89/mode/1up
2 CONT *cher,(d) who held four Knights fees ; but died S. P.  3. Hugh, a priest. 4. Ralph. 5. Richard. Having translated his seat from Etingdon to Shirley, in Derbyshire, he was the first of that family that called himself de Shirley. 
2 CONT *   HENRY, eldest son, held five Knights fees in Derbyshire, of Robert de Ferrers, Earl of Derby, and died about 1165. He gave the lordship of Ivanbrook to the Monks of Bildewas ; and was a witness to the foundation Charter of Mer
2 CONC evalle Abbey. He left issue by Joanna, daughter and heir of John de Clinton,(e) of Effex, 
2 CONT *   SEWALLIS, his son and heir, who, in 1167, acknowledged himself to hold of William de Ferrers, Earl of Derby, those nine Knights fees which Henry, his father, and his uncle, some time held of Earl Robert, grandfather to the sai
2 CONC d Earl. (f)He married Isabel, daughter and coheir of Robert Meynell, of Langley Meynell, Co. Derb. by whom he had a daughter, Elizabeth, wife of John de Walton, son of Simon Bishop of Norwich : and a son and heir, 
2 CONT *   Sir JAMES DE SHIRLEY, who was a Knight, and had freewarren granted to him in all his demesnes at Shirley in 1247, and at Etingdon in 1255. He married Agnes de Walton, daughter of Simon de Walton, Bishop of Norwich, and had iss
2 CONC ue by her Sir Ralph, his successor.(g) He died about 1278. 
2 CONT *   Which Sir RALPH de Shirley, in 7 Edw. I. held the manor of Eatendon(h) aforesaid, in com. Warwick, of Edmund Earl of Lancaster, the King's brother, by the service of two Knights fees. In 9 Edw. I. he was of full age. In 28 Edw
2 CONC . I he had the custody of the counties of Salop and Stafford, with the castle of Shrewsbury, committed to his charge ; and was Sheriff of the counties of Derby and Nottingham, in the 27th, 28th, and 30th of Edw. I. In 1301, he was
2 CONC  summoned to attend the King at Berwick upon Tweed, on Midsummer-day, well-appointed with horse and arms, to march against the Scots. In 3 Edward II. he was constituted 
2 CONT **	(d) Of Ednesour, co. Derb. 
2 CONT **	(c) Ever since the marriage with the heiress of Clinton, the family have taken her arms ; viz. Paly of six, Or, and Arg. a quarter of Bretaigny ; the family of Clinton, being allied to the Dukes of Bretaigny. 
2 CONT **	(f) See Lib. Nig. Scacc. under Derbyshire, 
2 CONT **	(g) Nichols gives also three younger sons ; James, Simon, and Henry, a priest, parson of St. George, co. Norf. 
2 CONT **	(h) He held also the manor of Barnham, co. Suff. near Thetford, by gift of his grandfather, Bishop Simon Walton. 
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/peerageofengland04colluoft#page/90/mode/1up 
2 CONT *one of the Justices in the county of Warwick for the gaol-delivery ; .... etc.
2 CONT *   He married (i)Margaret, daughter, and one of the coheirs of Walter de Waldeshief, of Fairfield, co. Derby, cupbearer to Edward II. and dying in 1327, 20 Edward II. left issue 
2 CONT *   THOMAS,(k) his son and heir, said to be "the great founder of the family of the Shirleys, famous in his time for his valour, and for the many services, &c. rendered to the Kings of England against the French."  A Commissioner 
2 CONC for assessing and collecting a fifteenth and tenth, granted in 11 Edw. III. and in the 12th, appointed to collect the scutage due to the king for the Scotch expedition. In 14 Edw. III. he served as one of the Knights in parliament
2 CONC  for the county of Warwick. This Sir Thomas Shirley died(l) in 36 Edw. II. 1362. 
2 CONT *   He married Isabel, daughter of Ralph, son and heir of Ralph Lord Basset, of Drayton, and sister and sole heir to her brother Ralph, the last Lord Basset of that line, who died 13 Richard II. without issue ; having by deed, dat
2 CONC ed Jan. 26th, 13 Richard II. named Sir Hugh Shirley, son of this Sir Thomas, by his sister Isabel, to be his nephew and right heir: thereby leaving him heir to his whole inheritance, on condition that he assumed his surname of Bas
2 CONC set, and his arms, leaving his own ; but should he refuse to comply with that condition, then the whole estate to go to the earl of Stafford, on the same terms, who was descended from Margaret Basset, great aunt to the said Lord B
2 CONC asset ; and should that Earl refuse to comply with the above condition, then the estates to go to the other relations mentioned in the will ; but expressly on condition that they assumed the name and arms of 
2 CONT **	(i) Dugdale's Antiq. of Warwicksh. p. 466. 
2 CONT **	(k) Nichols mentions an elder brother, Ralph de Shirley, by a former wife, who died S. P. 
2 CONT **	(l) He was noted also for the liberal donations of lands and rents by himself and his wife, to the College of St. Mary, at the Newark, in Leicester, &c. 
2 CONT **	(m) He and his Lady were interred in the Chapel of the Duke of Lancaster's College, called Newark. See Nichols, I, 399. III. 708. 
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/peerageofengland04colluoft#page/91/mode/1up
2 CONT *Basset.(n) But neither Sir Hugh Shirley, nor the Earl of Stafford, complied with the conditions of the will, but contended for the estate ; which dispute was not finally settled till the reign of Henry VI. when the estates of the
2 CONC  Barons Basset of Drayton, were divided between them. Colston Basset, in com. Nott. &c. 
2 CONT **	(n) This marriage with the heiress of Lord Basset, of Drayton, deserves some remarks. The Lady's legitimacy has latterly been doubted, in defiance of what seems decisive proof, for reasons which appear extremely weak, or rather
2 CONC  no reasons at all. .... etc.
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/peerageofengland04colluoft#page/92/mode/1up
2 CONT *being allotted to Shirley ; and Drayton Basset, com. Staff. &c. to Stafford. His widow remarried Sir Gerard Braybroke, Knt. 
2 CONT *   But to return : HUGH Shirley, son and heir of Sir Thomas, by Isabel Basset, succeeded his father ; and was, as before mentioned, by the will of the late Lord Basset his uncle, acknowledged by him to be his nephew and right hei
2 CONC r ; in 7 Rich II. he confirmed the manors of Shirley and Hoone, in com. Derby, and that of Etingdon, in com. Warwick, to his mother Isabel, then the wife of Sir Gerard Braybroke, Knt. these manors having been assigned to be for he
2 CONC r dower by Sir Thomas Shirley, his father. This Sir Hugh was made Chief Warden of Higham Ferrers Park, by John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster ; and in 22 Rich. II. constituted Constable of Donnington-Castle, by Henry of Bolingbroke, 
2 CONC Duke of Lancaster, afterward King Henry IV. On March 27th, 1400, being then a Knight, he was made Grand Falconer to King Henry IV. for the Kingdom of Ireland. He was killed at the battle of Shrewsbury,(o) on the part of King Henry
2 CONC  IV. being one of those who was habited as the King, and taken for him by the opposite party. By Beatrix his wife, sister and heir to John de Braose, or de Breus, of West-Neston (now called Wiston), in Sussex, heir male of the anc
2 CONC ient family of that name, Barons of Brember, in Sussex, and of Brecknock, Abergavenny, and Gower, in Wales, he had issue three daughters ; Isabel, wife of Sir John Cokayne, of Ashbourne ; Elizabeth ; and Nichola ; also 
2 CONT *   RALPH, his son and heir, then twelve years of age ; who, in 5 Henry V. was retained to serve that King in person in his army in Guyen, with six men at arms, and eighteen archers ; and the next year, with eight men at arms, and
2 CONC  sixteen archers, and was about that time knighted ; for in 8 Henry V. being then Sheriff of the counties of Nottingham and Derby, he was then styled a Knight. He was one of the chief Commanders under King Henry V. at the battle o
2 CONC f Agincourt, as appears by an ancient Roll in the office of Arms ; and was often a great actor in the subsequent Wars of the said King Henry V. in France ; as is evidently proved by diverse instruments of accord made between the s
2 CONC aid King and Sir Ralph Shirley ; in one of which, dated 1416, after agreement had for the number and pay of his soldiers, &c. the King granted to him all the prisoners that he or his soldiers should take, only reserving to himself
2 CONC , the French King, his 
2 CONT **	(o) The Spirits of valiant Shirley, Stafford, Blount, are in my arms." Prince of Wales's Speech in Shakespeare's Henry IV. 
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/peerageofengland04colluoft#page/93/mode/1up
2 CONT *adversary; the Dauphin, his son ; and all other Kings, his adversary's assistants, giving to him only the third part of the ransom of the captive Kings, by him or his soldiers taken. To this Sir Ralph, the feoffees of Ralph Lord 
2 CONC Basset, released all their right to the estates he claimed as heir to that Barony. In 1432, he resided at Radcliffe upon Soar ; and died at his government and charge in France, about 1443. His body was brought to England, and buri
2 CONC ed in the Collegiate Church of the Newark, at Leicester. His second wife was Alice, daughter of Sir John Cokayne, Knt. who died 1466, without issue. 
2 CONT *   By his first wife, Joan, daughter and heir of Thomas Basset, of Brailsford, co. Derb. he had a daughter, Beatrix, wife of John Brome, of Badesley Clinton, co. Warw. and 
2 CONT *   RALPH, who was Constable of Melbourn Castle, and of the castle in the Peak of Derbyshire : and died in 1466, "seised of many goodly manors, fair possessions, and large territories in the several counties of Leicester, Derby, W
2 CONC arwick, and Nottingham."  He was buried in the church of Brailesford, where his tomb still remains. 
2 CONT *   His first wife was Margaret, daughter and sole heir of John de Staunton, of Staunton Harald, in Leicestershire (whereby he obtained that estate, still the chief seat of the family), by Joan, daughter and coheir of Sir Ralph Me
2 CONC ynell, of Langley Meynell (with which family a former match of Shirley has been already, mentioned). By this marriage he had issue John, his son and heir, hereafter mentioned. 
2 CONT *   His second wife was Elizabeth, daughter of Sir John Blount, Knt. and sister to Walter Blount, Lord Montjoy ; by whom he had Ralph Shirley, Esquire of the Body to King Henry VII. ancestor to the Shirleys of Wiston, of Sussex ; 
2 CONC of whom an account will be given in an accompanying note, this branch having been of considerable eminence.(p) 
2 CONT *   His third wife was Lucia, daughter of Sir John Aston, of 
2 CONT **	(p) Ralph Shirley, by his second wife, Elizabeth Blount, sister to Walter Lord Mountjoy, had issue Sir Ralph Shirley, of Wiston, who, by Jane, daughter of Thomas Bellingham, of Lymster, in Sussex, Esq. had four daughters ; Jane
2 CONC , wife of John Dawtrey, of Petworth, in Sussex ; Elizabeth, wife of John Lee, of Fitleworth, co. Sussex ; Beatrix, wife of Edward Eldrington, of Hoggeston, and afterwards of Sir Edward Bray, of Vachery, Surrey, died 1582 ; and Isa
2 CONC bel, wife of John Dawtrey, of Hampton ; also, '''1. Sir Richard''', 2. Thomas Shirley, of West Grinsted, who died 1606; leaving by Elizabeth, daughter and coheir of Marmaduke Gorges, of Gloucestershire, Cecilie, daughter and cohei
2 CONC r, aet. 19, 1606, wife of Sir George Snelling, of Postlade, Sussex. 
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/peerageofengland04colluoft#page/94/mode/1up
2 CONT *Atherton, Knt, widow, first, of Sir John Byron, of Clayton and Colwich ; and, secondly, of Sir Barton Entwissel, Knt. Viscount of Brykbeke, in Normandy. She died in Feb. 1481 ; and lies buried at Braylesford. 
2 CONT *   John, son and heir, married Eleanor, daughter of Sir Hugh Willoughby of Wollaton, co. Notts, and dying 1485, left issue, 
2 CONT *   RALPH, twenty-six years old, who, for his valour in the battle of Stoke, in com. Nott. June 16th, 1487, was made a Banneret ; to which battle (q)he brought forces to the King's aid, when the Earl of Lincoln was slain. In 7 Hen
2 CONC ry VII. he was retained to serve the King in his wars beyond sea for one year ; and died on Jan. 6th, 1516-7, at his manor house of Staunton-Harold ; and was interred in Geronden Abbey. By his last will and testament,(r) which bea
2 CONC rs date four days before his death, writing himself Sir Rauf Shirley, of Staunton-Harold, in com. Leic. Knt. he orders his body to be buried at the discretion of his executors. He bequeaths to Jane his wife, his manors of Shirley 
2 CONC and Brailesford, with the lands, rents, and services, as also other lands, in full of her jointure and dower, for term of her life ; and his manor of Barnham, to the monastery of Geronden, for the term of fifty years. 
2 CONT **   Sir Ralph, the eldest son by his second wife, daughter of Sir Richard Guildeforde, had four daughters ; '''but by his first wife, Anne, daughter of John Shelley, of Michelgrove, he had''' Elizabeth, wife of John Michell, of S
2 CONC taunton ;  Anne, wife of Richard Fernwold ; Cecely, married to John Leedes ; Alice, married to Thomas Chandler, of Lyndfield. John ; Edward ; '''and 
2 CONT **   William Shirly, of Wiston, son and heir, who died May 29th, 1551 ; leaving by Mary, daughter of Thomas Isley, Esq. of Sundridge, Kent, Anthony Shirley, a younger son, of Preston, in Sussex ; whose great grandson, Sir Anthony,
2 CONC  was created a Baronet, 1665 (and left a granddaughter, Anne, married to Robert Western, of London, merchant), and 
2 CONT **   Sir Thomas Shirley, of Wiston, son and heir, Treasurer for the Wars in the Low Countries, from which he was removed 1597. (See Birch's Elizabeth, I. 455, and Sydney Papers, II.28, 31, 33.) By Anne, daughter of Sir Thomas Kemp
2 CONC e, he left issue several daughters, and three sons ; 
2 CONT ***	1. Sir Thomas. 
2 CONT ***	2. Sir Anthony, born 1563 ; a great traveller, died in Spain about 1636. See Fuller's Worthies, Sussex, 107. Hakluyt, Purchas, &c. 
2 CONT ***	3. Sir Robert, equally famous with his brother. See a whole-length print of him in Harding's Cabinet, &c. &c. He married Teresia, a relation of the great Sophy. 
2 CONT **   Sir Thomas married Frances, sister of Sir Thomas Vavasor, Knt. and left issue 
2 CONT **   Sir Thomas Shirley, M. D. who suffered much for his loyalty, and had the estate at Wiston torn from him by Sir John Fagg, Bart. See Topogr, IV. 335, 336.
2 CONT ** .... etc.
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/peerageofengland04colluoft#page/95/mode/1up
2 CONT *   It also appears by his will, that he had five brothers; and that he was possessed of the manors of Staunton-Harold, Rakedale, and Willowes, Burton, Long-Whatton, Ratclyff, Dunton, Esterleyke, Sutton-Bonyngton, and Newton-Regis
2 CONC  ; he bequeaths all his household furniture, plate, &c. to his wife and his son Francis, to be divided equally between them; and ordains executors, his cousin, Sir Richard Sackvil (to whom he bequeaths a cross of gold, hanging at 
2 CONC his chain) : his brother, Robert Hasylryg (husband to Elizabeth, his sister) ; Sir James Smith, his priest ; and Thomas Herbert. 
2 CONT *   He married four wives,(s) but had no issue by his first and third; and by his second wife, Elizabeth, daughter and coheir to Thomas Walsh, of Wanlip, in Leicestershire, had only a daughter, Anne, heir to her mother, married to
2 CONC  Sir Thomas Pultney, of Misterton, in com. Leic. Knt. ancestor to the late Earl of Bath. By his last wife, Jane, daughter to Sir Robert Sheffield, Knt. ancestor to the late Duke of Buckingham, he had Francis, his son and heir, bef
2 CONC ore mentioned. 
2 CONT *   Which FRANCIS was (t)Sheriff of the counties of Warwick and Leicester, in 4 Philip and Mary; and having lived(u) to an advanced age, famous for his charity and hospitality, died on July 27th, 1571 , and was buried in the churc
2 CONC h of Breedon on the Hill, in Leicestershire, where a monument was erected to the memory of him, and Dorothy his wife, who survived him but a short time; as appears by her last will and testament,(x) bearing date August 9th, 1571, 
2 CONC and the probate thereof May 16th following. She was daughter of Sir John Gifford, of Chillington, in Staffordshire, Knt. and married to her first husband, John Congreve, Esq; but had issue by the said Francis Shirley, three sons ;
2 CONC  John Shirley, Esq. hereafter mentioned; Edward, who died young; and Ralph : also three daughters ; Cassandra, married to Walter Powtrell, of West-Hallum, in com. Derb. Esq. ; Elizabeth, to Thomas Cotton, of Conington, in Huntingd
2 CONC onshire, Esq. father by her to the famous Sir Robert Cotton, Knt. and Bart, the great collector of the records now reposited in the British Museum; and Anne, to John Brook, of Madeley, in Shropshire, esq. 
2 CONT *   JOHN Shirley, eldest son and heir apparent, died A. D. 1570, in 
2 CONT **	(s) His second wife was Anne, daughter of Sir Henry Vernon, of Haddon, whom he married 1496 ; and his third, Anne, daughter of Thomas Warner, Esq. 
2 CONT **	(t) Fuller's Worthies. 	(u) Ex inscript. tumul. 
2 CONT ** 	(x) Ex Regist. Daper. qu. 16. Collect, T. Meller, Gent.  ___________________________________________________
2 CONT *	'Shirley1'
2 CONT *Ralph Shirley of Westneston (Wiston) - continued above
2 CONT *Visitation shows this Ralph as married also to Elizabeth, dau of Thomas Blount, with a note (apparently provided The Harleian's Society's editor) saying that she was "added, but in a like hand". On the basis that this looks like 
2 CONC a repetition of his mother (whose marriage to his father is mentioned in BP1934) we ignore this.
2 CONT *  m. Jane Belingham (dau of Thomas Belingham of Lemyster)
2 CONT **'''	1. Sir Richard Shirley of Westneston or Wiston (d 1543-4)
2 CONT **'''	  m1. Ann Shelley (dau of John Shelley of Michelgrove)
2 CONT ***		A. William Shirley of Westneston or Wiston
2 CONT ***		  m. Mary Isley (dau of Thomas Isley or Issley of Kent)
2 CONT ****			i. Sir Thomas Shirley of Westneston or Wiston (a 1585)
2 CONT ****			A web site gives Sir Thomas's dates as 1542-10.1612 and reports that he also married a Frances Vernon (probably of the family of Hodnet) who was mother of Sir Anthony. Visitation reports only one wife ...
2 CONT ****			  m. Anne Kempe (dau of Sir Thomas Kempe of Wye by Catherine Chenye)
2 CONT *****				a. Sir Thomas Shirley of Wiston (b 1564, d c1620)
2 CONT *****				  m. Frances Vavasor (dau of Sir Thomas Vavasor)
2 CONT *****				Visitation merely names Thomas and his wife. A web site confirms he inherited Wiston, gives his dates, and reports that he was father of ...
2 CONT ******					(1) Henry Shirley (dramatist, younger son)
2 CONT *****				b. Sir Anthony Shirley ## note probable duplication below
2 CONT *****				  m. Barbara Walsingham (dau of Sir Thomas Walsingham)
2 CONT *****				c. Robert Shirley
2 CONT *****				d. Mary Shirley
2 CONT *****				  m. Sir John Croftes of Suffolk
2 CONT *****				e. Elizabeth Shirley
