2 CONT
2 CONT Mary of Habsburg, also named Mary, Maria, or Marie of Hungary, of Austria, of Castile, or of Burgundy (18 September 1505 – 18 October 1558) was the Queen consort of Louis II of Hungary and Bohemia[1], and later governor of the Net
2 CONC herlands for her brother, Emperor Charles V.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Queen of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 Governor in the Netherlands
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 Ancestry
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 5 References
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born in Brussels to Philip I of Castile ("the Handsome") and Joanna "The Mad" of Castile. Her paternal grandparents were Maximilian I, Holy Roman Emperor, and Mary, Duchess of Burgundy. Her maternal grandparents were King 
2 CONC Ferdinand II of Aragon and Queen Isabella I of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria was a younger sister of Eleanor of Habsburg, Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor, Isabella of Habsburg and Ferdinand I, Holy Roman Emperor. She was an older sister of Catherine of Habsburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Queen of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis II of Hungary and Bohemia, the 20-year-old king, who died at the Battle of Mohács.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before Maria closed her first year of life, she was promised as a wife to the first son to be born to Ladislaus II of Bohemia and Hungary and his fourth wife Anne de Foix. This son was born in 1506 and was to become Louis II of Hu
2 CONC ngary and Bohemia.
2 CONT
2 CONT They were married on 13 January 1522 in Buda. Their joint portrait still exists. Both his robes and her alleged wedding dress are on display at the National Museum of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria served as Queen consort of Hungary and Bohemia for four years and seven months. On 29 August 1526, Louis was killed in the Battle of Mohács while leading his forces against Suleiman the Magnificent of the Ottoman Empire. The
2 CONC y were childless. The joined crowns of Hungary and Bohemia passed to her brother Ferdinand.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was an arranged marriage, but it became a happy one. After the death of Louis she continuously mourned him till her death. After the battle of Mohács, Nicolaus Olahus, secretary of Louis, attached himself to the party of King F
2 CONC erdinand I, but retained his position with the queen-dowager. She rejected every marriage proposal and always wore the heart-shaped medallion that was worn by her husband in the fatal battle of Mohács.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Punishment of Tityus painted by Titian for Maria's château de Binche, 1548-49 (Prado)
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Governor in the Netherlands
2 CONT
2 CONT the old Queen
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria would not marry again. Her chance at personal rule came four years later. Her paternal aunt Margaret of Austria died on 1 December 1530, leaving the position of Governor of the Seventeen Provinces vacant. Her brother Charles
2 CONC  established Maria as Margaret's successor in the Netherlands. She remained on the post until 1555. Charles gave her the castle of Binche south of Brussels, as a reward for her devoted service. She rebuilt it in conscious emulatio
2 CONC n of Fontainebleau; it was destroyed by Henry II of France's troops in 1554, but two of Titian's four paintings on the theme of punishments of rebels, commissioned in 1548 for Binche, are conserved at the Prado (illustration). She
2 CONC  was succeeded as Governor by Emmanuel Philibert, Duke of Savoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in Cigales. In her last will she requested that her heart-shaped gold medallion, once worn by her husband, be melted down and distributed among the poor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary was a keen collector art, and owned several important masterpieces of Early Netherlandish painting as well as more contemporary works. These included the Deposition of Christ by Rogier van der Weyden, now Prado, and the Arnol
2 CONC fini Portrait by Jan van Eyck, now National Gallery, London. Most of the collection passed to the Spanish Royal Collection after her death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Mary of Hungary was also a great patron of music. She supported both sacred and secular music at her court in the Netherlands, where her maître de chappelle was Benedictus Appenzeller. Several elaborate music manuscripts tha
2 CONC t she commissioned during her reign are preserved today in Spain in the monastery of Montserrat.
2 CONT
