2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLAND,%20Kings%201066-1603.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT King Henry VII had one possible illegitimate son[717] by a Breton lady:  
2 CONT
2 CONT 9.          [ROLAND de Velville ([1471/85]-1535).  Born while Henry Tudor was in exile in Brittany.  Constable of Beaumaris Castle, Anglesey 1509-1535.]  m AGNES Griffith, daughter of GWILYM Fychan of Penrhyn [Chamberlain of North
2 CONC  Wales] & his second wife Gwenllian daughter of Iowerth ap David (-1542).    
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         daughter.  m TUDUR ap Robert Fychan of Berain.  
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT He was knighted in 1497 and was Constable of Beaumaris Castle. He is sometimes presented as the clear "illegitimate issue" of Henry VII of England by "a Breton lady whose name is not known". The possibility this was Henry's illegi
2 CONC timate son is baseless.[6
2 CONT
2 CONT Roland de Velville From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Sir Roland de Velville Constable of Beaumaris Castle, Captain of Beaumaris Castle and Town, Anglesey (Ynys Môn), Wales Spouse(s)	Agnes Griffith Issue Grace de Velville Jane 
2 CONC de Velville (m. Robert Thomas ap Robert of Berain) Father	Henry VII of England, presumed Mother	unknown to historians (per Beauclerk-Dewar et al., Sir Roland's heraldry reflected known parentage) Born	1471/4 Brittany; under Franci
2 CONC s II, a duchy of France (per Beauclerk-Dewar et al., granted Letters of Denization, 1512) Died	25 June 1535 Beaumaris Castle, Wales Buried	Chapel of the Blessed Virgin Mary, Beaumaris Castle Sir Roland de Velville (1471/4 - 25 Jun
2 CONC e 1535, also Vielleville, Veleville, or Vieilleville)[1] is alternatively presented as the clear "illegitimate issue" (son) of King Henry VII of England by "a Breton lady whose name is not known",[2] or as a favored member of the 
2 CONC court of Henry VII and later recipient of beneficences, brought home to England with 28-year-old Henry after his exile in Brittany, an adolescent "of unknown parentage", and so a possible or likely illegitimate son, with formal hi
2 CONC storical opinion undecided.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Beauclerk-Dewar and Roger Powell note that while not having official position or office, de Velville was a courtier and "favourite of the king, participating in numerous jousts and accompanying the king out hunting."[1] He s
2 CONC aw military service in Brittany in 1489, and likely in France with the King in 1492, was knighted in 1497 after the Battle of Blackheath, and was one of a small group of knights individually rewarded for their efforts by Henry VII
2 CONC .[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Beauclerk-Dewar and Powell further state that historical records regularly place de Velville at royal tournaments marking significant royal events (marriages, visits of foreign monarchs) of the Court of Henry VII, in particular hi
2 CONC s jousts, from 1494-1507; in May 1509 he was appointed to attend Henry VII's funeral as a knight of the Royal Household, and in June to participate in the royal jousts in celebration of the accession of Henry VIII. Shortly thereaf
2 CONC ter, he was appointed Constable and Captain of Beaumaris Castle, Anglesey (Ynys Môn, in Wales) in 1509,[2] a position he held until his death.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT History writer Alison Weir notes "he married a Welshwoman, and had issue" [i.e., a child or children].[2] His wife Agnes (née Griffith), was widow of Robert Dowdyng and daughter in the powerful Welsh family of William (Gwilym) Gri
2 CONC ffith Fychan and so brother to Sir William Griffith, a Chamberlain of North Wales;[1] Agnes gave him two daughters, Grace and Jane, the latter who, in marriage to Tudor ap Robert Vychan, gave birth to Katherine Tudor, in Welsh Cat
2 CONC rin o Ferain (who is sometimes referred to as "the mother of Wales").[4] de Velville died on 25 June 1535, cause unknown, and was buried in the Chapel of the Blessed Virgin Mary in Beaumaris Castle.[1]
2 CONT
