2 NOTE The heraldic overlap between the House of Cabeljauw and the House of Chabot is a classic case of medieval armorial coincidence (or "convergent evolution") that requires careful distinction to avoid merging unrelated lineages.
3 CONT
3 CONT The Heraldic Conundrum: Three Fish or Three Lions?
3 CONT
3 CONT While both families were high-status, their arms are often confused in later transcriptions due to the visual similarity of their charges in stylized seals.
3 CONT
3 CONT House of Chabot (Poitou, France): Their name is a play on the French word for the "bullhead" fish (chabot). Their traditional arms are: Or, three bullheads (chabots) haurient gules (three red fish standing upright on a gold field)
3 CONC .
3 CONT
3 CONT House of Cabeljauw (Flanders): The name translates to "Codfish," yet ironically, the Flemish Cabeljauw family often utilized a variation of the Lions of Flanders. Their arms are frequently cited as: Or, three lions rampant sable (
3 CONC three black lions on a gold field).
3 CONT
3 CONT The Source of the Confusion
3 CONT
3 CONT The confusion typically arises from two specific historical factors:
3 CONT
3 CONT Canting Arms (Puns): Because both names refer to fish (Chabot/Bullhead and Cabeljauw/Cod), later heralds sometimes incorrectly "corrected" Cabeljauw seals to look like Chabot fish, assuming the family must have been using a pun on
3 CONC  their name.
3 CONT
3 CONT Louis de Cabeljauw's Knightly Status: As a Knight and Seigneur de la Woestyne, Louis de Cabeljauw operated in a sphere where French heraldic influence (from the Chabot territories in Poitou) and Flemish influence (from the Halluin
3 CONC s) met. In legal documents, the stylized tails of the Halluin/Cabeljauw Lions can sometimes be mistaken for the fins of a fish if the seal is poorly preserved.
3 CONT
3 CONT Louis de Cabeljauw vs. Chabot
3 CONT
3 CONT It is critical to distinguish Louis de Cabeljauw from the French Chabots because:
3 CONT
3 CONT Territorial Focus: Louis’s power was concentrated in the Woestyne and Cabeljauw estates of West Flanders, tied directly to the van Halewijn and van de Woestyne networks.
3 CONT
3 CONT The Halluin Marriage: His status as the husband of Adrienne van Halewijn (Dame de Cabeljauw) anchors him to the Flemish cadet branch, not the French Poitevin nobility.
3 CONT
3 CONT Corrected Timeline: By fixing his active period to c. 1475–1510, we separate him from the 12th-century "origins" where many of these heraldic myths began.
3 CONT
3 CONT V. Documents and Evidence
3 CONT
3 CONT User Summary & Corrections History (2026-01): Confirms the chronological correction that prevents the misattribution of the 15th-century Louis to earlier unrelated eras.
3 CONT
3 CONT Halluin-The Final Descent Records: Identifies the specific union of Louis and Adrienne as a local territorial consolidation within the Flemish nobility.
3 CONT
3 CONT Heraldic Comparative Study: Distinguished the Flemish "Cabeljauw" (Lions) from the French "Chabot" (Fish) to ensure the integrity of the Halluin-Cabeljauw descent.
2 NOTE The reason for the difficulty in locating "Cabeljauw" as a distinct armorial entry is that the name is a local Flemish translation of the French Chabot.
3 CONT
3 CONT In the context of Adrienne van Halewijn and the Louis de Cabeljauw correction, we are looking at a classic case of bilingual heraldry.
3 CONT
3 CONT The "Chabot" vs. "Cabeljauw" Connection
3 CONT
3 CONT The House of Chabot is one of the most ancient noble lineages of Poitou and France. In the Flemish archives of the late 15th century, the name was "Flemished" (vernacularized) to Cabeljauw (or Kabeljauw) because both words refer t
3 CONC o the same type of fish: the bullhead or chabot (and sometimes the cod, though heraldically it is consistently the bullhead).
3 CONT
3 CONT Heraldic Analysis: The Lion and the Fish
3 CONT
3 CONT The Original Blazon: Or, three chabots (bullhead fish) gules, placed 2 and 1.
3 CONT
3 CONT The Translation: In Roeselare or Brugge, a heraldic artist would record these as the arms of "Heer Louis Cabeljauw" because the visual pun (canting arms) only works if the name of the fish matches the name of the family.
3 CONT
3 CONT The Appearance: The fish are typically depicted haurient (vertical, with heads up) or paly (stacked vertically), with their distinctive large heads which characterize the "chabot" fish.
3 CONT
3 CONT Forensic Impact on the Bio
3 CONT
3 CONT By identifying the Cabeljauws as a branch or ally of the House of Chabot, the marriage of Adrienne van Halewijn moves from being a "local marriage" to an "International Power Alliance."
3 CONT
3 CONT Status Check: The Chabots were top-tier nobility in France. If Louis was using these arms in Flanders, it confirms that Adrienne’s husband was part of an elite "Administrative Knight" class that bridged the gap between the French 
3 CONC and Burgundian courts.
3 CONT
3 CONT The "Fish" in the Landbooks: When you see a reference in the landbooks to "Cabeljauw's land" or a seal with three red fish, you are looking at the Halluin’s connection to one of the most powerful bureaucratic families in the regio
3 CONC n.
3 CONT
3 CONT The "Bullhead" vs. "Cod": While "Cabeljauw" literally translates to "Cod" in modern Dutch, in 15th-century heraldry, the Chabot (bullhead) was the standard representation.
3 CONT
3 CONT Updated Forensic Note for Section III (Heraldry)
3 CONT
3 CONT Note on Canting Arms: The spouse of Adrienne van Halewijn, Louis de Cabeljauw, bore the arms of the House of Chabot (Or, three chabots gules). This is a linguistic translation within the archives: French Chabot = Flemish Cabeljauw
3 CONC . This identifies the family as part of the high-ranking administrative nobility serving the transition from Valois to Habsburg authority.
