2 CONT
2 CONT Added by Janet Milburn 1/31/21 (A rather humorous depiction of Henry IV) ________________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Henry III ''the Good'' King of Navarre''', also known as <br/>
2 CONT '''Henry IV ''the Great'' 1st Bourbon King of France''' also known as<br/>
2 CONT '''Henry the Co-Prince of Andorra
2 CONT
2 CONT '''==Links:==
2 CONT http://www.realmofhistory.com/2017/04/18/animation-reconstruction-henry-iv-france/
2 CONT *[http://thepeerage.com/p10139.htm#i101385 The Peerage]
2 CONT *[http://www.geneall.net/F/per_page.php?id=3254 Geneall]
2 CONT *[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_IV_of_France Wikipedia]
2 CONT *[https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hendrik_IV_van_Frankrijk in Dutch/Nederlands]<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry IV (French: Henri IV) (13 December 1553 – 14 May 1610), ruled as King of France from 1589 to 1610 and, as Henry III, King of Navarre from 1572 to 1610. He was the first monarch of the Bourbon branch of the Capetian dynasty i
2 CONC n France. His parents were Jeanne III of Navarre and her husband, Antoine de Bourbon, duc de Vendôme.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a Huguenot, Henry was involved in the Wars of Religion before ascending the throne in 1589. Once crowned, he changed his faith from Calvinism to Catholicism, but in 1598 he enacted the Edict of Nantes, which guaranteed religiou
2 CONC s liberties to the Protestants and thereby effectively ended the civil war. One of the most popular French kings, both during and after his reign, Henry showed great care for the welfare of his subjects and displayed an unusual re
2 CONC ligious tolerance for the time. He was murdered by a fanatical Catholic, François Ravaillac.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was nicknamed Henry the Great (Henri le Grand), and in France is sometimes called le bon roi Henri ("good king Henry") or le Vert galant ("the green gallant", a reference to his constant womanizing). He also gave his name to
2 CONC  the Henry IV style of architecture, which he patronized.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although baptized as a Roman Catholic, Henry was raised as a Protestant by his mother Jeanne d'Albret; Jeanne declared Calvinism the religion of Navarre. As a teenager, Henry joined the Huguenot forces in the French Wars of Religi
2 CONC on. On 9 June 1572, upon Jeanne's death, he became King Henry III of Navarre.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 18 August 1572, Henry married Marguerite de Valois, sister of King Charles IX. Henry's marriage was believed by most to be an effort to bring religious peace to the kingdom. However, leading Catholics (possibly including Cather
2 CONC ine de' Medici, mother of the bride) secretly planned a massacre of Protestants gathered in Paris for the wedding, which served as the lure. In the resulting Saint Bartholomew's Day Massacre, on 24 August, several thousand Protest
2 CONC ants were killed in Paris and thousands more in the countryside. Henry narrowly escaped death by pretending to convert to Roman Catholicism. He was kept in confinement, but escaped in early 1576; on 5 February of that year, he abj
2 CONC ured Catholicism at Tours and rejoined the Protestant forces in the military conflict.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henri of Navarre became the legal heir to the French throne upon the death in 1584 of François, Duke of Alençon, brother and heir to the Catholic King Henri III, who had succeeded Charles IX in 1574. Because Henry of Navarre was a
2 CONC  descendant of King Louis IX, King Henry III had no choice but to recognize him as the legitimate successor. Salic law disinherited the king's sisters and all others who could claim descent by the distaff line. However, since Henr
2 CONC y of Navarre was a Huguenot, this set off the War of the Three Henries phase of the French Wars of Religion. The third Henri, Duke Henri of Guise, pushed for complete suppression of the Huguenots, and had much support among Cathol
2 CONC ic loyalists. This set off a series of campaigns and counter-campaigns culminating in the battle of Coutras In December 1588 Henri III had Henry of Guise murdered, along with his brother, Louis Cardinal de Guise. This increased th
2 CONC e tension further, and Henry III was assassinated shortly thereafter by a fanatic monk.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of Henri III in 1589, Henri of Navarre nominally became the king of France. But the Catholic League, strengthened by support from outside, especially from Spain, was strong enough to force him to the south, and he had
2 CONC  to set about winning his kingdom by military conquest, aided by money and troops bestowed by Elizabeth I of England. The League proclaimed Henry's Catholic uncle Charles, the Cardinal de Bourbon, King as Charles X, but the Cardin
2 CONC al himself was Henry's prisoner. Henri was victorious at Ivry and Arques, but failed to take Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of the old Cardinal in 1590, the League could not agree on a new candidate. While some supported various Guise candidates, the strongest candidate was probably Infanta Isabella, the daughter of Philip II of Spain, 
2 CONC whose mother Elisabeth had been the eldest daughter of Henri II of France. The prominence of her candidacy hurt the League, which thus became suspect as agents of the foreign Spanish, but nevertheless Henry remained unable to take
2 CONC  control of Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the encouragement of the great love of his life, Gabrielle d'Estrées, on 25 July 1593 Henri declared that Paris vaut bien une messe ("Paris is well worth a Mass") (cite?) and permanently renounced Protestantism, thus earning 
2 CONC the resentment of the Huguenots and his former ally, Queen Elizabeth. However, his entrance into the Roman Catholic Church secured for him the allegiance of the vast majority of his subjects, and he was crowned King of France at t
2 CONC he Cathedral of Chartres on 27 February 1594. In 1598, however, he declared the Edict of Nantes, which gave circumscribed toleration to the Huguenots.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry's first marriage was not a happy one, and the couple remained childless. The two had separated, even before Henri had succeeded to the throne, in August, 1589 and Marguerite de Valois lived for many years in the chateau of U
2 CONC sson in Auvergne. After Henry had become king, various advisers impressed upon him the desirability of providing an heir to the French Crown, in order to avoid the problem of a disputed succession. Henri himself favored the idea o
2 CONC f obtaining an annulment of his first marriage, and taking Gabrielle d'Estrées as a bride, who had already borne him three children. Henry's councilors strongly opposed this idea, but the matter was resolved unexpectedly by Gabrie
2 CONC lle d'Estrées' sudden death in April 1599, after she had given birth prematurely to a stillborn son. His marriage to Marguerite was annulled in 1599, and he then married Marie de Médicis in 1600.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henri IV proved to be a man of vision and courage. Instead of waging costly wars to suppress opposing nobles, Henri simply paid them off. As king, he adopted policies and undertook projects to improve the lives of all subjects, wh
2 CONC ich made him one of the country's most popular rulers ever.
2 CONT
2 CONT A declaration often attributed to him is:
2 CONT
2 CONT “ Si Dieu me prête vie, je ferai qu’il n’y aura point de laboureur en mon royaume qui n’ait les moyens d’avoir le dimanche une poule dans son pot! ” 
2 CONT
2 CONT (God willing, I will ensure that there is no working man in my kingdom who does not have the means to have a chicken in the pot every Sunday!)
2 CONT
2 CONT This egalitarian statement epitomizes the peace and relative prosperity Henry brought to France after decades of religious war, and demonstrates how well he understood the plight of the French worker or peasant farmer. Never befor
2 CONC e had a French ruler even considered the importance of a chicken or the burden of taxation on his subjects, nor would one again until the French Revolution. After generations of domination by the extravagant Valois dynasty, which 
2 CONC had caused the French people to pay to the point of starvation for the royal family's luxuries and intrigue, Navarre's charisma won the day.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henri's forthright manner, physical courage and military success also contrasted dramatically with the sickly, effete languor of the last tubercular Valois kings, as evinced by his blunt assertion that he ruled with "weapon in han
2 CONC d and arse in the saddle" (on a le bras armé et le cul sur la selle).
2 CONT
2 CONT During his reign, Henri IV worked through his right-hand man, the faithful Maximilien de Bethune, duc de Sully (1560-1641), to regularize state finance, promote agriculture, drain swamps to create productive crop lands, undertake 
2 CONC many public works, and encourage education, as with the creation of the College Royal Louis-Le-Grand in La Flèche (today Prytanée Militaire de la Flèche). He and Sully protected forests from further devastation, built a new system
2 CONC  of tree-lined highways, and constructed new bridges and canals. He had a 1200 m canal built in the park at the Royal Château at Fontainebleau (which can be fished today), and ordered the planting of pines, elms and fruit trees.
2 CONT
2 CONT The king renewed Paris as a great city, with the Pont Neuf, which still stands today, constructed over the River Seine to connect the Right and Left Banks of the city. Henri IV also had the Place Royale built (since 1800 known as 
2 CONC Place des Vosges), and added the Grande Galerie to the Louvre. More than 400 meters long and thirty-five meters wide, this huge addition was built along the bank of the Seine River, and at the time was the longest edifice of its k
2 CONC ind in the world. King Henri IV, a promoter of the arts by all classes of peoples, invited hundreds of artists and craftsmen to live and work on the building’s lower floors. This tradition continued for another two hundred years, 
2 CONC until Emperor Napoleon I banned it. The art and architecture of his reign has since become known as the Henri IV style.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Henri's vision extended beyond France, and he financed several expeditions of Pierre Dugua, Sieur de Monts and Samuel de Champlain to North America that saw France lay claim to Canada.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although he was a man of kindness, compassion, and good humor, and was much loved by his people, he was the subject of many murder attempts (for example by Pierre Barrière and Jean Châtel). On 14 May 1610, King Henry IV was assass
2 CONC inated in Paris by a fanatically passionate Catholic, François Ravaillac, who stabbed the king to death while he rode in his coach. Henry was buried at the Saint Denis Basilica. Henry's widow, Marie de Médicis, served as Regent to
2 CONC  their 9-year-old son, Louis XIII, until 1617.
2 CONT
2 CONT The reign of Henry IV made a lasting impact on the French people living there for generations after. A statue of him was built in his honor the Pont Neuf in 1614, only four years after his death. Although this statue - as well as 
2 CONC those of all the other French kings - was destroyed during the French Revolution, it was the first to be rebuilt, in 1818, and it still stands today on the Pont Neuf. A cult surrounding the personality of Henri IV emerged during t
2 CONC he Restoration. The restored Bourbons were keen to downplay the contested reigns of Louis XV and Louis XVI, and instead emphasized the reign of the benevolent Henry IV. The song Vive Henri IV ("Long Live Henry IV") was used during
2 CONC  the Restoration, as an unofficial anthem of France, played in the absence of the king. In addition, when Princess Maria Carolina of the Two Sicilies gave birth to a male heir to the throne of France, seven months after the assass
2 CONC ination of her husband Charles Ferdinand, duc de Berry by a Republican fanatic, the boy was conspicuously called Henri in reference to his forefather Henry IV (see Henri, comte de Chambord). The boy was also baptized in the tradit
2 CONC ional way of Béarn/Navarre, with a spoon of Jurançon wine and some garlic, as had been done when Henry IV had been baptized in Pau, although this custom had not been followed by any Bourbon king after Henry IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Henri IV, né Henri de Bourbon (13 décembre 1553 à Pau - 14 mai 1610 à Paris) fut roi de Navarre (Henri III de Navarre, 1572-1610) puis roi de France (1589-1610), premier souverain français de la branche dite de Bourbon de la dynas
2 CONC tie capétienne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il était le fils de Jeanne III, dite Jeanne d'Albret, reine de Navarre et d'Antoine de Bourbon, chef de la maison de Bourbon, descendant du roi Louis IX et premier prince de sang[1]. En vertu de la « loi salique » cette filiation 
2 CONC fera d'Henri le successeur naturel du roi de France à la mort de François, duc d'Anjou (frère et héritier du roi Henri III), en 1584.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contemporain d'un siècle ravagé par les guerres de religion, il y fut d'abord lourdement impliqué en tant que prince de sang et chef protestant avant d'accéder au trône de France. Pour être accepté comme roi, il se convertit au ca
2 CONC tholicisme, et signa l'Édit de Nantes, énième traité de paix qui autorisait tout en la limitant la liberté de culte pour les protestants mais mit fin aux guerres de religion. Il fut assassiné le 14 mai 1610 par un fanatique, Franç
2 CONC ois Ravaillac, rue de la Ferronnerie à Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sommaire
2 CONT
