2 CONT
2 CONT Died 1577 in England
2 CONT
2 CONT  Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Shelley of Mapeldurham, son of Sir William Shelley and Allic Belknap, husband of Mary Copley.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas was his parent's second son. Sir William Shelley, Justice of the Common Pleas, died seised of the manor of West Mapledurham in 1548. By his Will dated 6 November, 1548, he left this manor and all lands in Hampshire which he
2 CONC  had purchased of Sir John Roger to his son [this] Thomas, a recusant, in tail male. By an Inquisition (IPM) taken at Winchester 2 October, 1570, it was ascertained that Thomas Shelley, late of Mapledurham, had been a fugitive in 
2 CONC foreign parts beyond the seas since 1 December, 1558, and was then living in Louvain, and that before his departure he had granted a twelve years' lease of all his lands and tenements in Mapledurham to Thomas Goldforde and John Je
2 CONC rvys. He died seised of the manor in 1577, his heir being his son Henry, aged thirty-eight, whose name occurs five years later in a list of the prisoners for religion in the custody of Anthony Thorpe "keeper of the Whyte Lyon in S
2 CONC outhwarke". At this time the manor house was the refuge of numerous priests, who were always sure to find a welcome, a place to say their Mass, and if necessary a secure hiding-place; and there are many references to it in the cor
2 CONC respondence of the time.[2] Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT     ↑ Benolte, Thomas; Philipot, John; & Owen, George. The Visitations of the County of Sussex: 1530 and 1633-4. London: The Harleian Society, 1905. Vol LIII, pp 37, Shelley & 111, Copley.
2 CONT     ↑ https://www.british-history.ac.uk/vch/hants/vol3/pp85-93
2 CONT
2 CONT     The Visitations of Hampshire 1530, 1575, 1622 and 1634 edited by W.Harry Rylands, F.S.A., London, 1913, Visitation of Hampshire p.7, Shelley. 
