2 CONT *SHIRLEY, Thomas I (c.1542-1612), of Wiston, Suss.
2 CONT *b. c.1542, 1st s. of '''William Shirley of Wiston by Mary, da. of Thomas Isley''' of Sundridge, Kent. educ. Oriel, Oxf, 1554, BA 1557; G. Inn 1559. m. Ann, da. of Sir Thomas Kempe of Olantigh, Kent, 3s. inc. Thomas II 7da. suc. f
2 CONC a. 1551.1 Kntd. 1573.
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1558-1603/member/shirley-thomas-i-1542-1612 ___________________
2 CONT *Sir Thomas Shirley (c.1542 – October 1612),[1] of Wiston in Sussex, was an English Member of Parliament, government official and courtier who is said to have suggested the creation of the rank of baronet.
2 CONT *Thomas Shirley was the eldest of the three children of William Sherley (c.1498–1551) of Wiston, Sussex, and Mary Isley, the daughter of Thomas Isley of Sundridge, Kent.[1]
2 CONT *He was knighted in 1573, and served as High Sheriff of Surrey and Sussex in 1576. Also in 1573, he began rebuilding the family seat of Wiston House, which he turned into a massive country house. (It is now the site of the governm
2 CONC ent's Wilton Park conference centre.)
2 CONT *However, soon afterwards Shirley found himself in considerable financial difficulties which eventually swallowed the family fortune. In 1586 Queen Elizabeth I appointed Sir Thomas Treasurer-at-War to the English forces serving in
2 CONC  the Netherlands during the Dutch Revolt. This gave him the power to speculate with the funds that passed through his hands (a normal perk of office at this period), but he so mishandled them that he contracted massive debts to th
2 CONC e Crown and found himself accused of fraud. His estate, including Wiston House, was sequestered in 1602, though he continued to live at Wiston until his death in October 1612.
2 CONT *Sir Thomas was elected to Parliament as MP for Sussex in 1584, representing the county for many years, and then Steyning, in 1601 and 1604, which was controlled by Shirley as a pocket borough; he was apparently not distinguished 
2 CONC as a member, but tried to draw upon an MP's privilege of immunity from arrest in 1604 when his debts grew too pressing to meet. Despite his protests that he had parliamentary privilege, he was arrested at the instigation of a gold
2 CONC smith to whom he owed money, and placed in the Fleet Prison. The House of Commons of England made a number of attempts to order his release by issuing writs of Habeas Corpus, but the Warden of the Fleet Prison would not free him, 
2 CONC unless he received assurance that he would not himself be held liable for Shirley's debts, or blamed for what might technically be seen as allowing an 'escape'. The Commons had the Warden placed in the Tower of London and sent the
2 CONC  sergeant-at-arms of the Commons, who was carrying the mace, to the Fleet Prison to set Shirley free. However, the Warden's wife proved equally obdurate, and the sergeant-at-arms had to report that his mission had been a failure. 
2 CONC The Warden had originally been able to move around within the Tower, but now the Commons made sure that he was placed in an unpleasant dungeon, called the Little Ease, four feet (1.2 metres) square, within the Tower. This persuade
2 CONC d the Warden to release Shirley, and he also had to apologise on his knees to the House of Commons. Shirley then resumed his seat as an MP. Parliament subsequently passed a general act (The Privilege of Parliament Act), which conf
2 CONC irmed the privilege of freedom from arrest for Members, but also gave creditors an opportunity to recover what they were owed when the debtor ceased being an MP. This case is generally regarded as having finally settled the questi
2 CONC on of privilege from arrest in the Commons’ favour, and was cited as Sir Thomas Shirley's Case for centuries afterwards.
2 CONT *His sons Thomas, Anthony and Robert were all noted adventurers.
2 CONT *Shirley married, about 1559, Anne Kempe (c.1542–1623), the daughter of Sir Thomas Kempe (d.1591) of Wye, Kent, by whom he had twelve children, of whom three died as infants. Shirley was survived by three sons and six daughters:[1
2 CONC ][2]
2 CONT ** Sir Thomas Shirley (1564–1633/4), who married firstly Frances Vavasour, by whom he had three sons, including the playwright, Henry Shirley, and four daughters, and secondly Judith Bennett, the daughter of William Bennett of Lon
2 CONC don, and the widow of a husband surnamed Taylor, by whom he had five sons and six daughters.[3][4]
2 CONT ** Sir Anthony Shirley (1565–1636?), who married Frances Vernon (baptised 1573), the daughter of Sir John Vernon of Hodnet, Shropshire.[5][4]
2 CONT ** Robert Shirley (c.1581–1628), who married Sampsonia (c.1590–1668), the daughter of Isma‘il Khan, by whom he had an only son, Henry Shirley.[6]
2 CONT ** Margaret Shirley, who married Thomas West, 3rd Baron De La Warr (1577-1618).
2 CONT ** Mary Shirley, who married Sir John Crofts of Saxham, Suffolk.
2 CONT ** Anne Shirley, who married John Tracy, 1st Viscount Tracy, of Toddington, Gloucestershire.[7]
2 CONT ** Elizabeth Shirley, who married Edward Onslow, esquire.
2 CONT ** Kathryn Shirley, who married Sir Pexsall Brocas.
2 CONT ** Jane Shirley, who married Sir John Shirley (1569-1631) of Isfield, Sussex.
2 CONT ** Henry Shirley, Died as an infant.
2 CONT ** Eleanor Shirley, Died as an infant.
2 CONT ** Edward Shirley, Died as an infant.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Shirley_(died_1612) ___________________________________________________
