1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_of_Hohenlohe-Neuenstein
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip of Hohenlohe-Neuenstein (17 February 1550 – IJsselstein, 6 March 1606); buried at Öhringen, 5 november 1606, Count of Hohenlohe-Langenburg, was a Dutch army commander. Philip was the son of Ludwig Kasimir von Hohenlohe-Wald
2 CONC enburg and Anna zu Solms-Lich. On 7 february 1595 he married Maria of Nassau at Buren. The marriage was childless, but shortly before his death Philip adopted the nine-year old Margrita Maria, countess of Falckenstein.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip of Hohenlohe, also called Hollock, was an army commander in service of the Dutch Republic. He had been in service of William I of Orange since 1575, and he was William's Lieutenant-general in Holland. Hohenlohe conquered Ge
2 CONC ertruidenberg in 1576, Steenbergen in 1577, Tholen and Breda and later amongst others Mengen and Gennep (1599). In 1590 he constructed a fort at 's-Hertogenbosch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Because of his experience he remained lieutenant-general on the request of the States of Holland after William of Orange was assassinated in 1584. Maurice of Orange, William's son, was still too young to rule. Hohenlohe was a cour
2 CONC ageous, but reckless commander. He also had a drinking problem. After Maurice became captain-general of the Republic, relations between him and Hohenlohe gradually became worse. Maurice thought Hohenlohe was unreliable and was bad
2 CONC  at keeping secrets. The bad relations also had a private cause; In 1582 Hohenlohe had asked William of Orange for permission to marry his eldest daughter, Maria of Nassau. The marriage had not taken place yet because of financial
2 CONC  problems. After William's assassination there was a quarrel over his inheritance. The rightful heir of William of Orange was Philip William of Orange, but he was imprisoned in Spain. Maria had maintained control over William's po
2 CONC ssessions since 1584, and after the siege of Breda Maria apparently had managed to collect enough money to proceed with the marriage. After the marriage Hohenlohe was supposed to take control of an important share of the inheritan
2 CONC ce of William of Orange. Maurice opposed this, and in 1591 the States-General split the possessions. Maria of Nassau was given the county of Buren, Maurice was given the rest. Years of legal battle produced no results. In 1595 Hoh
2 CONC enlohe married with Maria of Nassau.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hohenlohe served under Maurice during Maurice's campaign of 1597, and during the Siege of Groenlo. But after his marriage Hohenlohe became more and more isolated. Maurice hardly gave him any command in his army anymore. He reached
2 CONC  a low-point in 1600, when Maurice dismissed him as lieutenant-general of Holland and Zeeland.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1604 Hohenlohe became ill and started getting paralysis-symptoms. In 1606 he died. After his death his body was taken to Öhringen (Baden-Württemberg) by his wife, and he was interred in the family-tomb in the collegiate church 
2 CONC of St. Peter and St. Paul.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference: [http://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Hohenlohe-Neuenstein-6 WikiTree Genealogy] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Oct 4 2016, 9:18:41 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Links:==
2 CONT
2 CONT *[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_of_Hohenlohe-Neuenstein Wikipedia]
2 CONT
