1 OCCU 2nd Prince of Gavere; Count of Egmont; Knight of the Golden Fleece
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Philippe d'Egmont was reared in the tragic shadow cast by his father. Philippe wasn't just a nobleman; he was the heir to a legacy of execution and confiscation. His father, Lamoraal, Count of Egmont, did not flee—
2 CONC he stayed to face the storm, a decision that ended at the executioner’s block in Brussels in 1568.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippe’s life was a decades-long mission to restore the honor and the shattered estates of the House of Egmont through absolute loyalty to the Spanish Crown—a path that led him directly to his marriage with Margaretha van Halewi
2 CONC jn.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Philippe d’Egmont; 2nd Prince of Gavere; Count of Egmont; Knight of the Golden Fleece'''
2 CONT
2 CONT “The Restorer of the Egmont Legacy”
2 CONT
2 CONT Active Period: c. 1558 – 1590.
2 CONT Status: Grandee of Spain; High-Noble of the Spanish Netherlands.
2 CONT Strategic Role: The Loyalist Successor. After the execution of his father, Philippe’s primary goal was the "Rehabilitation of the Bloodline." By serving the Spanish King with fervor, he successfully reclaimed the vast Egmont prope
2 CONC rties and titles that had been seized by the Duke of Alba.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biographical Confirmation:
2 CONT Birth Date: April 11, 1557 in Brussels. This confirms he was roughly 11 years old when his father was executed in 1568, a formative trauma that defined his unwavering loyalty to the Spanish Crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death Date: March 14, 1590.
2 CONT
2 CONT Location & Cause: Ivry-la-Bataille (near Ivry-sur-Seine). He died during the Battle of Ivry, a decisive conflict in the French Wars of Religion.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Pistoolschot" (Pistol Shot): This is historically accurate. Philippe was leading a contingent of Spanish-Flemish cavalry (supporting the Catholic League against Henry IV of France) when he was killed by a pistol shot during a cav
2 CONC alry charge.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''I. The Relation: Son of the Martyr'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Father: Philippe was the eldest son of Lamoraal, Count of Egmont, the legendary general and Flemish patriot.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Execution: Unlike other leaders like William the Silent who fled to Germany, Lamoraal remained in Brussels to welcome the Duke of Alba, believing his prior service to the King would protect him. He was arrested and beheaded fo
2 CONC r "treason" in the Grand Place.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Inheritance: Philippe inherited a title that was technically under "attainder" (legal forfeiture). His marriage to Margaretha van Halewijn (Gen 16) was a crucial part of his re-entry into the highest Catholic circles of the Ne
2 CONC therlands.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''II. Strategic Role: The Spanish Sword'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Unlike his father, who sought to bridge the gap between Flemish liberty and Spanish rule, Philippe chose a path of total alignment with Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Military Devotion: He served as a cavalry commander under Alexander Farnese (the Duke of Parma), fighting against the Dutch rebels to prove his house's loyalty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Property Restoration: Through his service and his high-status marriage to the Maignelay-Piennes branch of the Halluins, he successfully petitioned King Philip II to return the confiscated Egmont lands.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Prince of Gavere: He solidified the family’s princely status, ensuring that while the name "Egmont" was associated with a rebel, it was also associated with the most dedicated defenders of the Catholic faith.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''III. Heraldry & Visual Identity'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippe’s heraldry is a visual record of his father’s titles and his own restored status.
2 CONT
2 CONT Primary Shield: Chevronny or and gules (the famous Egmont chevrons).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Golden Fleece: As a Knight of the Golden Fleece, his shield is encircled by the most prestigious order of chivalry in Europe—a mark of favor his father had also held.
2 CONT
2 CONT Visual Note: In portraiture, Philippe is often depicted in full armor, emphasizing his martial role in the Spanish military, appearing every bit the "Spanish Grandee" his father was accused of not being.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''IV. Curator Logic: The "Redemption" Pivot'''
2 CONT
2 CONT [!IMPORTANT]
2 CONT Forensic Conclusion: Philippe represents "Dynastic Recovery."
2 CONT
2 CONT Curator Logic identifies Philippe as the vital counter-balance to his father’s tragedy. While Lamoraal is a hero of Flemish independence, Philippe is the architect of the family's survival. His marriage to Margaretha van Halewijn 
2 CONC linked the Egmonts to the Halluins’ untarnished Catholic reputation, providing the social "shield" necessary for the House of Egmont to endure the 16th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''V. Primary Sources (The "Auditors" Trail)'''
2 CONT * Généalogie de la Maison d’Egmont: Detailed records of the transition from Lamoraal to Philippe and the restoration of titles.
2 CONT * Archives du Conseil d'État (Brussels): Legal proceedings regarding the return of the Egmont estates in the 1570s.
2 CONT * Ernest Warlop, "The Flemish Nobility before 1300": Contextual background on the marriage alliances between the Halluins and the Imperial Flemish houses.
2 CONT * Military Dispatch (1590): Records of the Battle of Ivry detailing the fall of the Count of Egmont during the cavalry engagement.
2 CONT
2 CONT ⚠️ CAUTION: NAMESAKE COLLISION WARNING ⚠️
2 CONT
2 CONT Do not confuse this Philippe d'Egmont with his father's brother or later descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT THIS PROFILE: 2nd Prince of Gavere; husband of Margaretha van Halewijn.
2 CONT
2 CONT DO NOT CONFUSE WITH: His father, Lamoraal, whose story is often the focus of historical fiction and opera (Goethe/Beethoven).
2 CONT
2 CONT <span style="color:red; font-weight:bold;">This Biographical summary is drafted with AI assistance, cross-referenced with the House of Egmont and Halluin historical records. (This summary is intended for research leads only; cross
2 CONC -verification with original primary documentation is essential for final lineage certification.)</span>
