2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Tudor, Duke of Cornwall1
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #101488, b. 1 January 1511, d. 22 February 1511
2 CONT
2 CONT      Henry Tudor, Duke of Cornwall was born on 1 January 1511 at Richmond Palace, Richmond, London, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 He was the son of Henry VIII Tudor, King of England and Catarina de Aragón, Infanta de Aragón.
2 CONT
2 CONT  He died on 22 February 1511 at Richmond Palace, Richmond, London, England.1
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried at Westminster Abbey, Westminster, London, England.1
2 CONT
2 CONT      Henry Tudor, Duke of Cornwall gained the title of Duke of Cornwall on 1 January 1511.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry, Duke of Cornwall was the name of two sons of King Henry VIII of England and his first wife, Catherine of Aragon. Henry in total had six children by Catherine of Aragon; 2 girls, 3 boys, and 1 whose sex is unrecorded. Only o
2 CONC ne of their children, Princess Mary (later Queen Mary I) survived infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first Henry, Duke of Cornwall 
2 CONT
2 CONT The first Henry, Duke of Cornwall (1 January - 22 February 1511) was the second child, oldest son and Heir Apparent of King Henry VIII of England and Catherine of Aragon. He was born on 1 January, 1511, eighteen months after his p
2 CONC arents' marriage and coronation. His older sister, born 31 January 1510 was three months premature and stillborn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry and his queen planned extravagant celebrations rivalling that of his coronation for the birth of his son and heir, who immediately became Duke of Cornwall and was scheduled later in adulthood to become Prince of Wales, King 
2 CONC of England and third king of the House of Tudor. Henry carried Catherine's favour in a tournament, riding under the banner of "Sir Loyal Heart;" the relationship between the royal pair, already one of strong affection, had become 
2 CONC even more of a love match because of Catherine's success in providing a male heir. Known as "Little Prince Hal" and "the New Year's Boy," the prince was almost instantly beloved by the entire realm. However on 22 February 1511 the
2 CONC  young prince died suddenly. The cause of Henry's death was not recorded.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contemporary reports state that both parents were distraught at the loss of their second child and expected future king. The deeply religious Catherine spent many hours kneeling on cold stone floors praying, to the worry of courti
2 CONC ers. Henry distracted himself from his grief by unsuccessfully waging war against Louis XII of France with his father-in-law, Ferdinand II of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The second Henry, Duke of Cornwall
2 CONT
2 CONT The second Henry, Duke of Cornwall (December 1514) was the third son and fourth child of Henry VIII and Catherine of Aragon. Little is known about the prince, who died within one month of his birth.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The second Henry, Duke of Cornwall
2 CONT
2 CONT The second Henry, Duke of Cornwall (December 1514-) was the third son and fourth child of Henry VIII and Catherine of Aragon. Little is known about the prince, who died within one month of his birth.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT stillborn son Tudor, Duke of Cornwall1
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #101490, b. November 1513, d. November 1513
2 CONT
2 CONT      stillborn son Tudor, Duke of Cornwall was born in November 1513.1 He was the son of Henry VIII Tudor, King of England and Catarina de Aragón, Infanta de Aragón. He died in November 1513 stillborn.1 He was buried at Westminste
2 CONC r Abbey, Westminster, London, England.1
2 CONT
2 CONT      Stillborn son Tudor, Duke of Cornwall gained the title of Duke of Cornwall in November 1513.1
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry, Duke of Cornwall was the name of two sons of King Henry VIII of England and his first wife, Catherine of Aragon. Henry in total had six children by Catherine of Aragon; two girls, three boys, and one whose sex is unrecorded
2 CONC . Only one of their children, Princess Mary (later Queen Mary I) survived infancy.
2 CONT
