1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Georgia of Pomerania (1531–c. 1574) — the posthumous daughter of George I, Duke of Pomerania, Szczecin/Stettin and Wolgast, and Margaret of Brandenburg. — Polish Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT “Ladies of Człuchów Castle” — Wiktor Zybajło
2 CONT http://bibliotekacyfrowa.eu/Content/471/2002-01.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT In the history of the Człuchów region, the first woman to play a significant role was Georgia, the wife of the starost of Człuchów, Stanisław Latalski. A granddaughter of Anna Jagiellon, the sister of King Casimir Jagiellon, she w
2 CONC as born into the family of the Pomeranian dukes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father, George I, was the elder son of Bogusław X and Anna Jagiellon. In his time, he was regarded as an educated man. In 1508 he stayed at the court of the Duke of Saxony in Dresden, where he studied under eminent scholars. I
2 CONC n 1521 he visited Kraków in order, on behalf of his father, to ask King Sigismund the Old of Poland for protection over Western Pomerania, and above all for help in disputes with Brandenburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT George I died on May 11, 1537. A few months after his death, on November 28, his daughter from his second marriage, Georgia, was born. In accordance with Pomeranian custom, as a posthumous child she was given her father’s name. He
2 CONC r mother, Margaret, later remarried John, Prince of Anhalt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: “Wołogoski” means “of Wolgast,” not “Wallachia.” Also, the date 1537 in the Polish passage appears inconsistent with Georgia’s listed birth year of 1531 and may be a typo or OCR error in the source.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://thepeerage.com/p35489.htm#i354884
