0 @N6000000002188378874@ NOTE Bernabò Visconti
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Bernabò Visconti, also called Barnabò (1319 – 19 December 1385), was an Italian soldier and statesman, Lord of Milan in the 14th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born in Milan, the son of Stefano Visconti and Valentina Doria. From 1346 to 1349 he lived in exile, until he was called back by his uncle Giovanni Visconti.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1350 Bernabò married Beatrice (also known as Regina) della Scala, daughter of Mastino II of Verona, and forged both a political and aesthetic alliance between the two cities. His intrigues and ambitions kept him at war almost c
1 CONC ontinuously with Pope Urban V, the Florentines, Venice and Savoy. In 1354, at the death of Giovanni, he inherited the power of Milan, together with his brothers Matteo and Galeazzo. Bernabò received the eastern lands (Bergamo, Bre
1 CONC scia, Cremona and Crema), who bordered the Veronese territories. Milan itself was to be ruled in turn by the three brothers.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1356, after having offended the emperor, he pushed back a first attack to Milan by the imperial vicar Markward von Raudeck, imprisoning him. In 1360 he was declared heretic and condemned by Emperor Charles IV. The ensuing confl
1 CONC ict ended with a dismaying defeat at San Ruffillo against the imperial troops under Galeotto I Malatesta (July 29, 1361). In 1362, after the death of his sister's husband, Ugolino Gonzaga, caused him to attack also Mantua. Warring
1 CONC  on several different fronts, in December of that year he sued for peace with Urban V, through the mediation of King John II of France. However, having Barnabò missed to return the Papal City of Bologna and to present himself at A
1 CONC vignon, on March 4, 1363 he was excommunicated, together with his children, one of which, Ambrogio, was captured by the Papal commander Gil de Albornoz. With the peace signed on March 13, 1364, Visconti left the occupied Papal lan
1 CONC ds, in exchange of the raising of the ban and of 500,000 florins.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Spring 1368 Visconti allied with Cansignorio della Scala of Verona, and attacked Mantua, then ruled by Ugolino Gonzaga. The situation was settled later in the year through an agreement between him and emperor. Two years later h
1 CONC e besieged Reggio, which he managed to acquire from Gonzaga in 1371. The following war against the Este of Modena and Ferrara raised again the Papal enmity against the Milanese.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1373, the pope sent two papal delegates to serve Bernabò and Galeazzo their excommunication papers (consisting of a parchment bearing a leaden seal rolled in a silken cord). Bernabò, infuriated, placed the two papal delegates u
1 CONC nder arrest and refused their release until they had eaten the parchment, seal, and silken cord which they served him.[citation needed] He managed to resist, despite also the outbreak of a plague in Milan, whose consequences he su
1 CONC ppressed with fanatic energy.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1378 he allied with the Republic of Venice in its war against Genoa. His troops were however defeated in September 1379 in the Val Bisagno.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bernabò, whose dispotism and taxes had enraged the Milanese, was deposed by his nephew Gian Galeazzo Visconti in 1385. Imprisoned in the castle of Trezzo, he was poisoned in December of that year.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bernabò was an ally of Stephen II, Duke of Bavaria: three of his daughters were married with Stephen's descendants:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Viridis (d.1414) married Leopold III, Duke of Inner Austria and were the parents of Ernest, Duke of Austria the father of Frederick III, Holy Roman Emperor of Austria.
1 CONT     * Agnes (Executed 1391) married Francesco I Gonzaga
1 CONT     * Taddea (1351-81) married Stephen III, Duke of Bavaria. She was mother to Louis VII of Bavaria and Isabeau of Bavaria.
1 CONT     * Marco (d.1382)
1 CONT     * Valenza (d.1393) married Peter II of Cyprus
1 CONT     * Catherine (d.1404) married Gian Galeazzo
1 CONT     * Lucia (d.1424) Betrothed 1. Louis II of Anjou and married 2. Edmund Holland, 4th Earl of Kent
1 CONT     * Maddalena ( married Frederick, Duke of Bavaria and was mother of Henry XVI of Bavaria.
1 CONT     * Mastino (d.1404) married Antonia della Scala (d.1400)daughter of Cangrande II della Scala.
1 CONT     * Anglesia (d.1439) married Janus of Cyprus
1 CONT     * Giammastino married Cleofa (d.1403) daughter of Cangrande II della Scala.
1 CONT     * Elisabetta married Ernest, Duke of Bavaria and was the mother of Albert III, Duke of Bavaria.
1 CONT     * Antonia (1360-1405) married Eberhard III, Count of Württemberg
1 CONT     * Aymonette married Louis I de Berton des Balbes
1 CONT
1 CONT His illegitimate offspring were as follows:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Sagromoro married Achiletta Marliani.
1 CONT     * Donnina married John Hawkwood.
1 CONT     * Lancelloto
1 CONT     * Soprana wife of Giovanni de Prato
1 CONT     * Carlo Visconti (d. 1391 or 1403) married Beatrice daughter of John II of Armagnac.
1 CONT     * Lodovico (d.1385) married Violante Visconti.
1 CONT     * Ginevra married Leonardo Malaspina (d.1441)
1 CONT     * Palamede
1 CONT     * Isotta (d.1388) married Ludwig von Landau (d.1389)
1 CONT     * Ambrogio (Killed 1373)
1 CONT     * Ettore (d.1413) married Margherita Infrascati.
1 CONT     * Beroarda wife of Giovanni Suardi
1 CONT     * Enrica
1 CONT     * Riccarda wife of Bernard, seigneur de La Salle (d.1391).
1 CONT
1 CONT The funerary monument of Bernabò Visconti, with an equestrian statue, was made in 1363 by Bonino da Campione for the church of San Giovanni in Conca, together with that of his consort. They now stand in the Castello Sforzesco in M
1 CONC ilan.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Pizzagalli, Daniela (1994). Bernabò Visconti. Milan: Rusconi. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
