0 @N6000000002188309052@ NOTE Isabeau of Bavaria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabeau of Bavaria
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabeau de Bavière (also Isabella of Bavaria-Ingolstadt; c. 1371 – September 24, 1435) was a Queen Consort of France (1385-1422) after marrying Charles VI of France, a member of the Valois Dynasty, on July 17, 1385. She assumed a 
1 CONC prominent role in public affairs during the disastrous later years of her husband's reign.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Lineage
1 CONT     * 2 Career
1 CONT     * 3 Legacy
1 CONT     * 4 Children
1 CONT     * 5 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Lineage
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabeau of Bavaria was the daughter of Stephen III of Bavaria-Ingolstadt and Taddea Visconti.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her paternal grandparents were Stephen II, Duke of Bavaria (a son of Emperor Louis IV) and Elisabeth of Sicily (whose name Isabella received), daughter of king Frederick III of Sicily and his wife Eleanor of Anjou.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eleanor was herself a daughter of Charles II of Naples and Maria Arpad of Hungary. Maria was a daughter of Stephen V of Hungary and Elizabeth of the Cumans (whose namesake her great-granddaughter, and through that, ultimately Quee
1 CONC n Isabella became). Elizabeth was daughter of Kuthen of the Cumans, a chieftain apparently descending from the Kipchaks and lord of the clan of Kun which had settled to Hungary after Mongol pressure drove them westwards.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her maternal grandparents were Barnabò Visconti, Lord of Milan and Regina-Beatrice della Scala. Regina was daughter of Mastino II della Scala, Lord of Verona from 1329 to 1351 and his wife Taddea di Carrara.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Career
1 CONT Christine de Pisan presents her book to Queen Isabeau. She and her ladies wear jewelled heart-shaped stuffed or hollow "bourrelets" on top of hair dressed in horns; the walls are decorated with a combination of the French Fleurs-d
1 CONC e-lys and Isabeau's Wittelsbach arms.
1 CONT Christine de Pisan presents her book to Queen Isabeau. She and her ladies wear jewelled heart-shaped stuffed or hollow "bourrelets" on top of hair dressed in horns; the walls are decorated with a combination of the French Fleurs-d
1 CONC e-lys and Isabeau's Wittelsbach arms.
1 CONT Isabeau of Bavaria. Tomb effigy.
1 CONT Isabeau of Bavaria. Tomb effigy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabeau of Bavaria was the prominent and unpopular Queen of an unsuccessful reign. She assumed an unusually powerful role in government to fill the gap left by her husband's frequent bouts of insanity. Around this time she organis
1 CONC ed the disastrous Bal des Ardents, or 'Ball of the Burning Men'. She was named Regent due to her husband suffering greatly from what now is believed to have been schizophrenia, and she successfully replaced herself with a Royal mi
1 CONC stress, Odette de Champdivers. Her husband was never the wiser, and rarely made any public appearances.
1 CONT
1 CONT Others who vied for power in the place of the King included the King's brother Louis of Valois, Duke of Orléans, and their cousin John the Fearless, Duke of Burgundy. Queen Isabeau's strong partisanship for the Duke of Orléans led
1 CONC  to rumors of an extramarital affair. Orleans' bitter feud with Burgundy reached a crisis point when the former was assassinated in 1407. Bitter resentment continued and the late Duke's supporters became known as the Armagnacs.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry V of England took advantage of French internal strife and invaded the northwest coast. He delivered a crushing defeat to the French at Agincourt. Nearly an entire generation of military leaders died or fell prisoner in a sin
1 CONC gle day. John the Fearless, still feuding with Queen Isabeau, remained neutral as Henry V conquered towns in northern France.
1 CONT
1 CONT Most of Isabeau's twelve children did not survive to adulthood. Shortly after her fifth and final son assumed the title of dauphin as Heir to the Throne, the sixteen-year-old future Charles VII of France negotiated a truce with Jo
1 CONC hn the Fearless in 1418. Armagnac partisans murdered John while the two met on a bridge under Charles's guarantee of protection.
1 CONT
1 CONT The new Duke of Burgundy Philip the Good entered an active alliance with the English. With most of northern France under foreign domination, Isabeau agreed to the Treaty of Troyes in 1420. This arranged the marriage of her daughte
1 CONC r Catherine of Valois to Henry V and assigned the French Royal Succession to Henry V and their children. Isabeau's detractors and the Dauphin's political enemies cited this treaty as evidence that he was not the legitimate son of 
1 CONC Charles VI. The treaty did not have its intended effect on the French Royal Succession but did have an ultimate effect on English Royal Succession. Catherine's second marriage resulted in the eventual Tudor dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT Both Charles VI and Henry V died within two months of each other in 1422. Charles VII, now fully grown, claimed that the Treaty of Troyes was illegal and assumed leadership of the Armagnac party, ruling what was left of central an
1 CONC d southern France, and taking his father's former mistress, Odette de Champdivers, as his own.
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabeau and her son Charles VII shared no apparent love for each other. Charles was to face a similar relationship with his own son Louis XI. Charles' principal female mentor was his childhood guardian Yolande of Aragon, who refus
1 CONC ed to let him to go to Court when his mother summoned him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabeau moved to English-controlled territory and exerted no further influence over public affairs. She died in Paris in 1435 and is interred in the Saint Denis Basilica.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Legacy
1 CONT
1 CONT Posterity has not been kind to Isabeau of Bavaria. A popular saying late in her life was that France had been lost by a woman and would be recovered by a girl. Many took this to be a prediction of Joan of Arc.
1 CONT
1 CONT In fairness to Isabeau it must be noted that her leadership confronted double prejudice as a woman and a foreigner. There are a few bright spots in her reign, such as her artistic patronage. Isabeau aided the era's most significan
1 CONC t French author Christine de Pizan and sponsored artisans who developed innovative techniques in decorative arts.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Charles, Dauphin of Viennois (1386-1386)
1 CONT     * Jeanne (1388-1390)
1 CONT     * Isabella (1389-1409); m.1 Richard II of England; m.2 Charles, Duke of Orléans
1 CONT     * Jeanne (1391-1433); m. John VI, Duke of Brittany
1 CONT     * Charles, Dauphin of Viennois, Duke of Guyenne (1392-1401)
1 CONT     * Marie, Prioress of Poissy (1393-1438)
1 CONT     * Michelle (1395-1422); m. Philip III, Duke of Burgundy
1 CONT     * Louis, Dauphin of Viennois (1397-1415); m. Marguerite of Burgundy the Dauphin in Shakespeare's Henry V
1 CONT     * John, Dauphin of Viennois, Duke of Touraine (1398-1417); m. Jacqueline, Countess of Hainault and Holland
1 CONT     * Catherine, Queen of England, (1401-1438); m.1 Henry V of England; m.2 Sir Owen Tudor
1 CONT     * Charles VII of France, King of France, (1403-1461) m. Marie of Anjou - the Dauphin in Shakespeare's Henry VI
1 CONT     * Philip (1407-1407)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
