0 @N6000000002176643152@ NOTE He acted as regent for his father, Charles VI, from 1418, succeeding --
1 CONT --partially with the aid of Joan of Arc -- in driving the English from French
1 CONT soil, and, in solidifying the administration of the monarchy. His worst
1 CONT difficulties at first were of a financial nature: insufficient tax revenues.
1 CONT But he soon gained financial independence skillfully and energetically,
1 CONT in much the same fashion as was displayed in putting down a rebellion in 1440.
1 CONT All in all, he contributed much to the restoration of unity to his country.
0 @N6000000002188309158@ NOTE Charles VII of France
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles VII the Victorious
1 CONT King of France (more…)
1 CONT Detail from a portrait of Charles VII, by Jean Fouquet, tempera on wood, Louvre Museum, Paris, c. 1445
1 CONT Reign 21 October 1422 – 22 July 1461
1 CONT Coronation 17 July 1429
1 CONT Titles Duke of Touraine, Duke of Berry, Count of Poitou (1417–22)
1 CONT Dauphin de Viennois: As heir (5 April 1417 – 21 October 1422;
1 CONT as King (21 October 1422 – 3 July 1423);
1 CONT title maintained until 17 July 1429
1 CONT
1 CONT 'King of Bourges' (1422–29)
1 CONT Born 22 February 1403(1403-02-22)
1 CONT Paris, France
1 CONT Died 22 July 1461 (aged 58)
1 CONT Mehun-sur-Yèvre, France
1 CONT Buried Saint Denis Basilica
1 CONT Predecessor Charles VI
1 CONT Successor Louis XI
1 CONT Consort Marie of Anjou (1404–63)
1 CONT Issue Louis XI (1423–83)
1 CONT Yolande, Duchess of Savoy (1434–78)
1 CONT Magdalena, Princess of Viana (1443–95)
1 CONT Charles Duke of Berry (1446–72)
1 CONT Royal House Valois Dynasty
1 CONT Father Charles VI (1368–1422)
1 CONT Mother Isabella of Bavaria-Ingolstadt (1370–1435)
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles VII (22 February 1403 – 22 July 1461), called the Victorious (French: le Victorieux) or the Well-Served (French:le Bien-Servi), was King of France from 1422 to his death, though he was initially opposed by Henry VI of Engl
1 CONC and, whose servants ruled much of France from Paris.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a member of the House of Valois, the son of Charles VI, whose succession to the throne was left questionable by the English occupation of northern France. He was, however, famously crowned in Reims in 1429 through the endea
1 CONC vours of Joan of Arc to free France from the English. His later reign was marked by struggles with his son, the eventual Louis XI.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Early life
1 CONT     * 2 The Maid of Orleans
1 CONT     * 3 Close of reign
1 CONT     * 4 Ancestors
1 CONT     * 5 Children
1 CONT     * 6 Mistresses
1 CONT     * 7 Charles VII in the Arts
1 CONT     * 8 Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life
1 CONT
1 CONT Born in Paris, Charles was the fifth son of Charles VI of France and Isabella of Bavaria-Ingolstadt. His four elder brothers — Charles (1386), Charles (1392–1401), Louis (1397–1415) and John (1398–1417) — had each held the title o
1 CONC f Dauphin of France, heir to the French throne, in turn; each had died childless, leaving Charles with a rich inheritance of titles, and little else besides.
1 CONT
1 CONT Almost immediately after his accession to the title of Dauphin, Charles was forced to face the threat to his inheritance, being forced to flee Paris in May 1418 after the soldiers of John the Fearless, Duke of Burgundy attempted t
1 CONC o capture the city. In the following year, Charles attempted to make a reconciliation between himself and the Duke, meeting him and swearing peace on a bridge at Pouilly, near Melun, in July 1419. This proving insufficient, the tw
1 CONC o met again on 10 September 1419, on the bridge at Montereau. The Duke, despite previous history, proved over-trusting in his young cousin, assuming the meeting to be entirely peaceful and diplomatic, and bringing with him only a 
1 CONC small escort; the Dauphin's men reacted to the Duke's arrival, however, by setting upon him and killing him. Charles's level of involvement remained questionable ever afterwards: although he claimed to have been unaware of his men
1 CONC 's intentions, it was considered unlikely by those who heard of the murder, and furthered the feud between the family of Charles VI and the Dukes of Burgundy. Charles himself was later required by treaty with Philip the Good, John
1 CONC 's son, to pay penance for the murder, but he never did so; nonetheless, it is claimed, the event left him with a lifelong phobia of bridges.
1 CONT
1 CONT In his adolescent years, Charles was noted for his bravery and style of leadership: at one point after becoming Dauphin, he led an army against the English, dressed in the red, white and blue that represented France; his heraldic 
1 CONC device was a mailed fist clutching a naked sword. However, two events in 1421 broke his confidence: he was forced, to his great shame, to withdraw from battle against Henry V of England; and his parents then repudiated him as the 
1 CONC legitimate heir to the throne, claiming that he was the product of one of his mother's affairs (for which she was notorious). Humiliated, and in fear of his life, the Dauphin had fled to the protection of Yolande of Aragon, the so
1 CONC -called Queen of the Four Kingdoms, in southern France, where he was protected by the forceful and proud Queen Yolande, who married him to her daughter, Marie.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the death of Charles's insane father, Charles VI, the succession was cast into doubt: if the Dauphin was legitimate, then he was the rightful heir to the throne, but if not, the heir was the Duke of Orleans, in English captivit
1 CONC y; in addition to which, the Treaty of Troyes, signed by Charles VI in 1421, ordered that the throne pass to Henry VI of England, the son of the recently deceased Henry V by Catherine of Valois, daughter of Charles VI. None of the
1 CONC  three candidates had an unquestionable claim to the throne; the English, however, being already in control of northern France, including Paris, were able to enforce their King’s claim in those parts of France they occupied. North
1 CONC ern France was thus ruled by an English regent to Henry VI based in Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles, unsurprisingly, refused to allow his nephew to succeed rather than himself, and claimed the title King of France for himself; however, by indecision and a sense of hopelessness, he failed to make any attempts to throw the
1 CONC  English out. Instead, he remained in southern France, where he was still able to exert some small amount of power, maintaining an itinerant court in the Loire Valley at castles such as Chinon, being customarily known as "Dauphin"
1 CONC  still, or derisively as "King of Bourges" (Bourges being the region where he generally lived), periodically considering flight to the Iberian Peninsula, and allowing the English to advance in power.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The Maid of Orleans
1 CONT French Monarchy
1 CONT Capetian Dynasty
1 CONT (House of Valois)
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip VI
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    John II
1 CONT John II
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Charles V
1 CONT    Louis I of Anjou
1 CONT    John, Duke of Berry
1 CONT    Philip the Bold
1 CONT Charles V
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Charles VI
1 CONT    Louis, Duke of Orléans
1 CONT Charles VI
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Isabella of Valois
1 CONT    Catherine of Valois
1 CONT    Charles VII
1 CONT Charles VII
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Louis XI
1 CONT    Charles, Duke of Berry
1 CONT Louis XI
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Charles VIII
1 CONT Charles VIII
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1429, however, came a change. Orleans had been under siege since October 1428; the English regent, the Duke of Bedford (uncle of Henry VI) was advancing into the Duchy of Bar, ruled by Charles's brother-in-law, Rene; the French
1 CONC  lords and soldiers loyal to Charles were becoming increasingly desperate; and in the little village of Domremy, on the border between Lorraine and Champagne, a teenage girl named Jeanne d'Arc ("Joan of Arc"), having been given a 
1 CONC divine mission by God, demanded of the Duke of Lorraine the soldiers and resources necessary to bring her to Chinon, and the Dauphin. Granted an escort of five veteran soldiers and a letter of referral to Charles by the governor o
1 CONC f Vaucouleurs, Robert Baudricourt, Jeanne rode to Chinon, where Charles was in residence, arriving there on 10 March.
1 CONT
1 CONT What followed would later pass into legend. When Jeanne arrived at Chinon, Charles—testing Jeanne's claim to recognize him despite having never seen him—disguised himself as one of his courtiers, and stood in their midst when Jean
1 CONC ne (who was herself dressed in men's clothing) entered the chamber. Jeanne, immediately identifying him, bowed low to him and embraced his knees, declaring "God give you a happy life, sweet King!" Despite attempts to claim that an
1 CONC other man was in fact the King, Charles was eventually forced to admit that he was indeed such. Thereafter Jeanne referred to him as "Dauphin" or "Gentle Dauphin" until he was crowned in Reims four months later. After a private co
1 CONC nversation between the two (during which, Charles later stated, "Joan the Maid of Lorraine"—so-called because she united France under one King—revealed herself to know secrets about himself that he had voiced only in silent prayer
1 CONC  to God), Charles became inspired, and filled with confidence. Thereafter he became secure in his intention to claim his inheritance by traveling to Rheims.
1 CONT
1 CONT One of the important factors that aided in the ultimate success of Charles VII was the support from the powerful and wealthy family of his wife Marie d'Anjou (1404–63), particularly his mother-in-law the Queen Yolande of Aragon. D
1 CONC espite whatever affection he had for his wife, the great love of Charles VII's life was his mistress, Agnès Sorel.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the French won the Battle of Patay, Charles was crowned King Charles VII of France on July 17, 1429, in Reims Cathedral. Over the following two decades, King Charles VII recaptured Paris from the English and eventually recov
1 CONC ered all of France with the exception of the northern port of Calais.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Close of reign
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles's later years were marked by increasing hostility between himself and his heir, Louis. Louis demanded real power to accompany his position as the Dauphin; Charles refused. Accordingly, Louis stirred dissent and made plots 
1 CONC in attempts to destabilise his father, and quarrelled with his father's mistress, Agnès Sorel, on one occasion driving her with a bared sword into Charles's bed, according to one source. Eventually, in 1446, after Charles's final 
1 CONC son, also named Charles, was born, the King banished the Dauphin to the Dauphiny. The two never met again; Louis thereafter refused the King's demands that he return to court, eventually fleeing to the protection of Philip the Goo
1 CONC d, Duke of Burgundy, in 1456.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1458, Charles became ill: a sore on his leg (an early symptom, perhaps, of diabetes or syphilis) refused to heal, and the infection in it caused a serious fever. The King summoned his son, the Dauphin, to him from his exile in 
1 CONC Burgundy; the Dauphin refused, and employed astrologers to foretell the exact hour of his father's death. The King lingered on for the next two and a half years: increasingly ill, but unwilling to die.
1 CONT
1 CONT Finally, however, there came a point in the July of 1461 when the King's physicians concluded that Charles would not live past August. Ill and weary, the King became delirious, convinced that he was surrounded by traitors loyal on
1 CONC ly to his son; under the pressure of sickness and fever, the King went mad. By now another infection in his jaw had caused a tumour or abscess in his mouth; the swelling of this became so large that, for the last week of his life,
1 CONC  Charles could swallow no food or water. Although he asked the Dauphin to come to his deathbed, Louis refused, instead waiting for his father to die at Avesnes, in Burgundy. Thus, at Mehun-sur-Yèvre, attended by his younger son, C
1 CONC harles, and aware of his son's final betrayal, the King starved to death. He died on 22 July 1461, and was buried, at his request, beside his parents in St Denis.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Charles VII's legacy is far overshadowed by the deeds and eventual martyrdom of Joan of Arc, he himself was also responsible for successes unprecedented in the history of the Kingdom of France. When he died, France was fo
1 CONC r the first time since the Carolingian Emperors united under one ruler, and possessed its first standing army, which in time would yield the powerful gendarme cavalry companies, notable in the wars of the sixteenth century; he had
1 CONC  also established the University of Poitiers in 1432, and his policies had brought some economic prosperity to his subjects. His rule as a monarch had at times been marked by indecisiveness and inaction, and his ending years marke
1 CONC d by hostility between himself and his son; nonetheless, it is to his credit that he left his kingdom in condition better than he had found it in.
1 CONT
