0 @N6000000002188342473@ NOTE Anne of Cleves
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Anne of Cleves
1 CONT Queen Consort of England
1 CONT Anne of Cleves, painted by Hans Holbein the Younger
1 CONT Born 22 September 1515(1515-09-22)
1 CONT Düsseldorf, Cleves
1 CONT Died 16 July 1557 (aged 41)
1 CONT London, England
1 CONT Consort January 6, 1540 - July 9, 1540
1 CONT Consort to Henry VIII of England
1 CONT Father John "the Peaceful," duke of Cleves
1 CONT Mother Maria von Jülich-und-Berg
1 CONT
1 CONT Anne of Cleves (22 September 1515–16 July 1557) (German: Anna von Jülich-Kleve-Berg) was the fourth wife of Henry VIII of England from 6 January 1540 to 9 July 1540.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Biography
1 CONT           o 1.1 Wedding preparations
1 CONT           o 1.2 A doomed marriage
1 CONT     * 2 In fiction
1 CONT     * 3 Historiography
1 CONT     * 4 Lineage
1 CONT     * 5 References
1 CONT     * 6 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Biography
1 CONT
1 CONT Anne was born at Düsseldorf[1], the daughter of John III, ruler of the Duchy of Cleves, who died in 1538. After John's death, her brother William became Duke of Jülich-Cleves-Berg, bearing the promising epithet "The Rich." In 1526
1 CONC , her elder sister Sybille was married to John Frederick, Elector of Saxony, head of the Protestant Confederation of Germany and considered the "Champion of the Reformation." At the age of 12 (1527), she was betrothed to Francis, 
1 CONC son and heir of the Duke of Lorraine while he was only 10, so the betrothal was considered 'unofficial.' While her brother William was a Lutheran, the family's politics made them suitable allies for England's King Henry VIII in th
1 CONC e aftermath of the Reformation, and a match with Anne was urged on the king by his chancellor, Thomas Cromwell.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Wedding preparations
1 CONT
1 CONT The artist Hans Holbein the Younger was dispatched to paint portraits of Anne and her sister, Amelia whom Henry was considering for the role of his fourth wife. While it was usual for court painters to be flattering in their portr
1 CONC ayal of important people, the only truly important person here was the King: Henry hired the artist to be as accurate as possible, not to flatter these sisters. Holbein was a superb portraiture artist and there's every reason to b
1 CONC elieve his attractive portrayal of Anne was true, since Holbein remained at court and continued to win royal commissions. The portrait is currently displayed in The Louvre in Paris.
1 CONT
1 CONT Negotiations with the Cleves Court were in full swing by March 1539. Cromwell oversaw the talks and a marriage treaty was signed on 4 October of the same year. While Henry valued education and cultural sophistication in women, Ann
1 CONC e lacked these in her upbringing; she received no formal education as a child, and instead of being taught to sing or play an instrument, she was skilled in needlework. She had learned to read and write, but in German only. Nevert
1 CONC heless, Anne was considered gentle, virtuous, and docile, qualities that made her a realistic candidate for Henry.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry, impatient to see his future bride, journeyed to Rochester on New Year’s Day 1540 and walked in on Anne unannounced. Without the benefit of an advance portrait to prepare her for Henry's appearance, she did not realise that 
1 CONC 'this man' was to be her future husband, so she did not curtsy—or even rise from her chair—when Henry entered the room. The king took an immediate dislike to her because of it and announced: "I like her not." Henry urged Cromwell 
1 CONC to find a legal way to avoid the marriage but, by this point, evading the marriage was impossible without offending the Germans.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] A doomed marriage
1 CONT The Six Wives of
1 CONT King Henry VIII
1 CONT Catherine of Aragon
1 CONT Anne Boleyn
1 CONT Jane Seymour
1 CONT Anne of Cleves
1 CONT Catherine Howard
1 CONT Catherine Parr
1 CONT
1 CONT The two were married on 6 January 1540 at the royal Palace of Placentia in Greenwich, London by Archbishop Thomas Cranmer, despite Henry's very vocal misgivings. If his bride had objections, she kept them to herself. The phrase “G
1 CONC od send me well to keep” was engraved around Anne’s wedding ring.
1 CONT
1 CONT Anne was commanded to leave the Court on June 24 and on July 6 she was informed of her husband's decision to reconsider the marriage. In a short time, Anne was asked for her consent to an annulment, to which she agreed. The marria
1 CONC ge was annulled on July 9, 1540, on the grounds both of non-consummation and of her pre-contract to Francis of Lorraine. She received a generous settlement, including Hever Castle, home of Henry's former in-laws, the Boleyns. Anne
1 CONC  of Cleves House, in Lewes, Sussex, is just one of many properties she owned; she never lived there. Made a Princess of England and called "the King's Beloved Sister" by her former husband, Anne remained in England for the rest of
1 CONC  her life.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] In fiction
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa Gregory's novel, The Boleyn Inheritance, is told from the viewpoint of three prominent women at the Tudor court of Henry VIII: Anne of Cleves, Catherine Howard and Jane Boleyn.
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret Campbell Barnes's My Lady of Cleves describes what Anne's life might have been like between the time her portrait was painted by Hans Holbein and when King Henry VIII died.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Historiography
1 CONT
1 CONT Sir Horace Walpole, writing in the 18th century resurrected the myth which described Anne as "The Flanders Mare" — a monument to ugliness. This view persisted, and it is still a popular stereotype. Most modern historians, however,
1 CONC  disagree with it, and the Holbein portrait certainly contradicts Walpole. Another point of view is that Anne found Henry repulsive because of his obesity, and set out to make him dislike her.
1 CONT
1 CONT Another theory suggests that they agreed that they simply did not get along well with each other — Anne had been raised in the small provincial court at Düsseldorf and shared none of the musical and humanistic literary tastes of H
1 CONC enry's court. Another theory suggests that shifts in a threatened Catholic French-Spanish alliance removed any diplomatic motivations for their union. Henry and Anne split on amicable terms. This theory is supported by the fact th
1 CONC at she received a good settlement.
1 CONT
1 CONT Finally, there is the theory that the marriage was politically inconvenient because of the growing hostility between Henry and the Duke of Cleves [2].
1 CONT
