0 @N6000000005598980475@ NOTE rumored to be gayPhilip represented the most formidable Catholic powerinEurope.He was a devote Roman Catholic. He was kin to QueenMary's motherand he was an inevitable pick for Mary and thusthe two were married.Philip had a motiva
1 CONC tion to stay in hismarriage to the queen mainlybecause he was determined to gaincontrol of the government, and to bringEngland within theImperial Hapsburg orbit. Mary put all of her trust inPhilip,somewhat foolishly.During the cou
1 CONC rse of their marriage, Mary'shealth declined andPhilip was already attempting to make a bond betweenhimself andElizabeth. There is some indication to the fact that Philipwasanxious, and excited to see Elizabeth the night Mary sent
1 CONC  forher inher chambers. Tradition has told us that the strainedconversation betweenMary and Elizabeth that evening wasoverheard by Philip. Soon after, theproposal of marriage wasmade to Elizabeth, before Queen Mary was dead.Althou
1 CONC gh,  Philipoffered his hand to Elizabeth, he was politely refused.Hisconcentration was to insure that the country would continued toreignunder Catholicism, but most of all he couldn't let go ofhis desire to runthe government.Engli
1 CONC sh support of the Dutch rebels and their persistentattackson Spanish shipping led Philip to plan the invasion of Englandin1588. However, the ãA“Invincible ArmadaãA”  wassignificantlydefeated.Philip was not the bloodthirsty tyrant 
1 CONC portrayed byhis enemiesand by later writers.  He did not trust even his mostloyalservants, and partly as a result his court was riddled withfaction.Isolated from reality, Philip lived and died in hisstrange court at theEscorial, n
1 CONC ever gaining the governmentcontrol that he so longed for.
